and there's like bugs crawling

263
00:12:03.260 --> 00:12:04.520
through the walls and stuff like that,

264
00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:08.023
that then your life is like sunshine and roses.

265
00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:10.650
That's not the case, man.

266
00:12:10.650 --> 00:12:11.980
It's not the case at all.

267
00:12:11.980 --> 00:12:13.470
It's not like, oh, I'm sober now.

268
00:12:13.470 --> 00:12:14.850
Life is perfect and great.

269
00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:15.797
And look how grand everything is

270
00:12:15.797 --> 00:12:17.473
and the sun shines everyday.

271
00:12:19.286 --> 00:12:21.310
Certainly, I don't know if you had this,

272
00:12:21.310 --> 00:12:23.420
but you do have the pink cloud,

273
00:12:23.420 --> 00:12:27.090
and that lasts longer for some people than for others.

274
00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:29.080
And I experience that.

275
00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:31.500
But then life sort of intervenes again.

276
00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:36.500
And you have problems that creep up

277
00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:38.468
like everybody else does.

278
00:12:38.468 --> 00:12:42.040
And in many ways, at least in my experience,

279
00:12:42.040 --> 00:12:47.040
it's oftentimes more difficult to manage that,

280
00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:50.470
because I'm like an emotional baby.

281
00:12:50.470 --> 00:12:52.610
Because I medicated myself for so long

282
00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:54.400
that when I finally got sober,

283
00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:59.040
I had the emotional maturity of a 17-year-old,

284
00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:01.330
when I started using and drinking.

285
00:13:01.330 --> 00:13:04.360
I hadn't been able to develop tools

286
00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:05.460
like a normal human being

287
00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:06.517
for how to manage that kind of stuff.

288
00:13:06.517 --> 00:13:09.030
And so I'm really sensitive.

289
00:13:09.030 --> 00:13:12.000
I get really bent out of shape by small stuff.

290
00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:15.892
And it can be very easily overwhelming for me.

291
00:13:15.892 --> 00:13:20.892
Yeah, that's my experience with sobriety is

292
00:13:21.030 --> 00:13:24.610
that sobriety is not the finish line.

293
00:13:24.610 --> 00:13:26.940
It's the starting line.
Exactly.

294
00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:28.930
Like you've gotten yourself far enough

295
00:13:28.930 --> 00:13:32.240
to get to the point where you're in good enough shape

296
00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:36.020
to do something that's incredibly hard.

297
00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:39.280
And man, I know exactly what you're talking about,

298
00:13:39.280 --> 00:13:41.510
'cause I meet people who'd tell me all the time,

299
00:13:41.510 --> 00:13:45.140
they're like, "Why do you still live like you're 16?"

300
00:13:45.140 --> 00:13:48.050
And I was like, "I still am 16."

301
00:13:48.050 --> 00:13:50.470
I'm starting from scratch.

302
00:13:50.470 --> 00:13:54.060
So at this point, you've been sober a couple years now,

303
00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:56.340
three years, two and a half years or something like that?

304
00:13:56.340 --> 00:13:58.480
Coming up on four years,

305
00:13:58.480 --> 00:14:00.600
which I never thought that I would do that.

306
00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:03.070
Which is huge, man, congratulations.

307
00:14:03.070 --> 00:14:03.903
It's amazing.

308
00:14:03.903 --> 00:14:07.890
And yet at the same time, you're still new.

309
00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:10.880
You're still new and the nerve endings are,

310
00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:12.020
there's probably a lot of nerve endings

311
00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:14.620
that are still really kind of raw.

312
00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:19.620
Yeah, one of the parts of finding ultra

313
00:14:19.930 --> 00:14:24.930
that I really loved was the slack period

314
00:14:25.190 --> 00:14:28.010
after you did the first Ultraman,

315
00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:29.510
because you're like,

316
00:14:29.510 --> 00:14:32.620
it's like I went out and did this ridiculous,

317
00:14:32.620 --> 00:14:37.590
grueling race, and now what?

318
00:14:37.590 --> 00:14:39.083
What's next?

319
00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:43.020
What do you do after that?

320
00:14:43.020 --> 00:14:44.963
And that's been my experience.

321
00:14:46.980 --> 00:14:48.950
I started running,

322
00:14:48.950 --> 00:14:51.620
I went out real hard and real strong

323
00:14:51.620 --> 00:14:56.600
with just running on anger, really,

324
00:14:56.600 --> 00:14:59.220
with no training and no idea of form,

325
00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:01.690
or what the right shoes were to wear,

326
00:15:01.690 --> 00:15:03.840
or what to eat, or any of that information.

327
00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:06.930
And then went out and overran and injured myself,

328
00:15:06.930 --> 00:15:09.610
and then had this awesome, massive year

329
00:15:09.610 --> 00:15:12.730
where I just did race after race, after race.

330
00:15:12.730 --> 00:15:17.630
And then I burnt out a little bit, and-

331
00:15:17.630 --> 00:15:18.800
Well, you kind of proved

332
00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:20.800
to yourself that you could do it,

333
00:15:20.800 --> 00:15:23.023
so you crossed a couple finish lines.

334
00:15:24.088 --> 00:15:26.820
And then you kind of get to that place where you're like,

335
00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:28.410
well, what does this mean to me?

336
00:15:28.410 --> 00:15:31.700
Why am I, what does it, why am I doing this?

337
00:15:31.700 --> 00:15:32.760
What's driving this?

338
00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:36.320
And what is important to me about this pursuit?

339
00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:38.670
Yeah, and that was one of the things is

340
00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:43.670
that for me, running was a tool, and it was just like,

341
00:15:46.520 --> 00:15:49.420
I was gonna, if, for example,

342
00:15:49.420 --> 00:15:50.600
if you're writing and you don't know

343
00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:54.480
where the story is gonna go, and you go out for a run,

344
00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:56.950
that's on your mind for the next four hours,

345
00:15:56.950 --> 00:16:00.580
or however long you're running, you figure it out.

346
00:16:00.580 --> 00:16:03.330
And I think that that's because it's on your mind,

347
00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:05.410
but also when you're running,

348
00:16:05.410 --> 00:16:08.500
you're able to engage a different part of your consciousness

349
00:16:08.500 --> 00:16:10.470
and kind of tap into an,

350
00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:13.060
you're unconscious on a certain level.

351
00:16:13.060 --> 00:16:15.190
In some ways, you're turning your brain off

352
00:16:15.190 --> 00:16:16.500
and you're kind of letting go,

353
00:16:16.500 --> 00:16:18.640
and it becomes that active meditation state

354
00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:22.340
that allows some of those creative pathways

355
00:16:22.340 --> 00:16:24.230
to engage in a different way.

356
00:16:24.230 --> 00:16:26.640
And I know when I'm struggling with something,

357
00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:27.810
whether it's a creative problem

358
00:16:27.810 --> 00:16:30.193
or just a life problem or an emotional problem.

359
00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:35.060
Oftentimes, I find the solution on a run

360
00:16:35.060 --> 00:16:36.780
in a way that I would've never been able to

361
00:16:36.780 --> 00:16:39.280
if I was grinding at home trying to figure it out.

362
00:16:40.180 --> 00:16:41.570
Do you remember those,

363
00:16:41.570 --> 00:16:43.393
this is probably like late '90s.

364
00:16:43.393 --> 00:16:45.820
There were like optical illusions

365
00:16:45.820 --> 00:16:47.390
that they would print in the newspaper.

366
00:16:47.390 --> 00:16:49.260
And what you had to do was look at it

367
00:16:49.260 --> 00:16:52.680
and then unfocus your eyes.

368
00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:54.603
And then you would see a 3D image.

369
00:16:56.120 --> 00:16:56.953
Vaguely.

370
00:16:56.953 --> 00:16:58.130
You mean like the lamp

371
00:16:58.130 --> 00:16:59.870
and the two faces kind of thing, or-

372
00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:02.950
No, it was more like,

373
00:17:02.950 --> 00:17:05.180
I'll figure out exactly what it is,

374
00:17:05.180 --> 00:17:06.640
but there's like an image there,

375
00:17:06.640 --> 00:17:08.700
but you can't see it if you look

376
00:17:08.700 --> 00:17:11.390
directly at it and if your eyes focus.

377
00:17:11.390 --> 00:17:15.010
So you have to focus your eyes on an imaginary point

378
00:17:15.010 --> 00:17:17.810
six inches in front of what you're looking at.

379
00:17:17.810 --> 00:17:21.370
And then you'll see a wizard riding a dolphin

380
00:17:21.370 --> 00:17:24.280
or something ridiculous like that.

381
00:17:24.280 --> 00:17:26.310
And that's been my experience

382
00:17:26.310 --> 00:17:30.770
with running is that when I go out,

383
00:17:30.770 --> 00:17:35.720
I'm concentrating on sort of the discreet task of like,

384
00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:40.210
okay, I've gotta keep my speed up,

385
00:17:40.210 --> 00:17:42.650
or I've gotta not run too fast, or I've gotta,

386
00:17:42.650 --> 00:17:45.660
so you're focusing on smaller things like that.

387
00:17:45.660 --> 00:17:50.350
And then while you're focusing on that,

388
00:17:50.350 --> 00:17:55.020
it's like, oh, I forgive my sister for that thing

389
00:17:55.020 --> 00:17:58.890
that she did when I was in fifth grade or whatever.

390
00:17:58.890 --> 00:18:02.367
You just get little gifts like that where it's like,

391
00:18:02.367 --> 00:18:05.050
oh yeah, I just figured that out.

392
00:18:05.050 --> 00:18:06.693
Oh, okay, cool.

393
00:18:07.930 --> 00:18:12.000
It's almost like you're plugging from the loops.

394
00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:14.277
I don't know about you, but I'll get into a rut,

395
00:18:14.277 --> 00:18:16.880
and my mind just loops on some thought.

396
00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:21.640
Whether it's a resentment or a conflict or whatever it is.

397
00:18:21.640 --> 00:18:24.980
And I'll just walk around obsessing on some little thing.

398
00:18:24.980 --> 00:18:26.923
And I can't unplug.

399
00:18:28.150 --> 00:18:30.930
And I have to change my environment,

400
00:18:30.930 --> 00:18:32.890
I have to change my behavior.

401
00:18:32.890 --> 00:18:36.120
And I almost can't will myself to stop thinking about it.

402
00:18:36.120 --> 00:18:39.070
I have to take an action that's going to shift that.

403
00:18:39.070 --> 00:18:43.500
And for me, the easiest thing to do is to go work out.

404
00:18:43.500 --> 00:18:45.223
That almost does it for me.

405
00:18:46.180 --> 00:18:51.180
Yeah, well, this actually bring up

406
00:18:51.210 --> 00:18:52.840
another question I wanna raise.

407
00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:57.820
A friend of mine, Jacob, who I lost in 2001,

408
00:18:58.670 --> 00:19:03.670
he was a pretty hardcore weightlifter and a heroin addict,

409
00:19:04.550 --> 00:19:07.613
which two things don't really go together.

410
00:19:09.650 --> 00:19:14.650
And I asked, I said, and I was a pretty hardcore drunk.

411
00:19:15.590 --> 00:19:18.617
And I was like, "How can you bear to go to the gym?"

412
00:19:19.830 --> 00:19:22.327
And he said,

413
00:19:22.327 --> 00:19:27.300
"It's the one thing that I know that removes

414
00:19:27.300 --> 00:19:29.843
that sort of nattering voice in your head,

415
00:19:31.340 --> 00:19:33.940
better than drink or drugs is,

416
00:19:33.940 --> 00:19:35.280
to just work out hard

417
00:19:35.280 --> 00:19:37.453
to the point where you're so exhausted.

418
00:19:38.390 --> 00:19:39.870
That voice is quieted,

419
00:19:39.870 --> 00:19:42.810
and it's quiet in your head for a second.

420
00:19:42.810 --> 00:19:43.890
There's no doubt about that.

421
00:19:43.890 --> 00:19:46.280
And when I was drinking,

422
00:19:46.280 --> 00:19:50.280
if I could somehow find my way to go for a run

423
00:19:50.280 --> 00:19:52.770
or do some kind of physical activity,

424
00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:56.650
that was fuel for my thinking that I didn't have a problem,

425
00:19:56.650 --> 00:19:57.879
because it's like, as long as I,

426
00:19:57.879 --> 00:20:00.980
if I can get to the gym even if for a half an hour

427
00:20:00.980 --> 00:20:04.370
and I get through that, then I'm cool.

428
00:20:04.370 --> 00:20:07.420
I don't have anything that I need to look at in the mirror.

429
00:20:07.420 --> 00:20:10.833
Yeah, man, I know that logic too well.

430
00:20:11.991 --> 00:20:14.280
I used to do the same thing with my drinking,

431
00:20:14.280 --> 00:20:17.330
where I was like, well, all right, I'll quit for a week.

432
00:20:17.330 --> 00:20:19.320
There, I quit for a week, I'm not an alcoholic.

433
00:20:19.320 --> 00:20:20.620
Let's go get some whiskey.

434
00:20:21.552 --> 00:20:23.370
Yeah, like I said to you

435
00:20:23.370 --> 00:20:25.540
before we started recording that,

436
00:20:25.540 --> 00:20:28.660
if I wasn't an alcoholic, I'd get drunk every night.

437
00:20:28.660 --> 00:20:30.380
Only an alcoholic thinks that way.

438
00:20:30.380 --> 00:20:32.110
And unless you're an alcoholic,

439
00:20:32.110 --> 00:20:34.950
it's hard to relate to that mentality.

440
00:20:34.950 --> 00:20:36.610
And I think it's something

441
00:20:36.610 --> 00:20:41.610
that is just distinct to that kind of personality.

442
00:20:42.050 --> 00:20:44.020
And you're the first person

443
00:20:44.020 --> 00:20:47.690
I've had on the show who is in recovery.

444
00:20:47.690 --> 00:20:48.987
And there's a lot of people have been like,

445
00:20:48.987 --> 00:20:51.580
"When are you gonna talk about the addiction stuff?"

446
00:20:51.580 --> 00:20:56.580
And I haven't really addressed it in audio format before.

447
00:20:56.710 --> 00:20:59.208
So this is like new territory.

448
00:20:59.208 --> 00:21:01.300
And I think it's important,

449
00:21:01.300 --> 00:21:04.360
and I'm at peace with talking about it,

450
00:21:04.360 --> 00:21:09.360
but it's also like, it's a vulnerable feeling.

451
00:21:09.770 --> 00:21:12.040
And I wanted to know what it was like for you

452
00:21:12.040 --> 00:21:13.420
when you were writing "The Long Run,"

453
00:21:13.420 --> 00:21:16.490
which is a fantastic work, by the way.

454
00:21:16.490 --> 00:21:18.820
You're a very gifted writer.
Thank you.

455
00:21:18.820 --> 00:21:21.070
And I love the story,

456
00:21:21.070 --> 00:21:25.740
and obviously I relate incredibly to your,

457
00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:29.410
it's the inner demon and grappling

458
00:21:29.410 --> 00:21:31.610
with the addiction that just,

459
00:21:31.610 --> 00:21:33.343
I can tap into immediately.

460
00:21:34.540 --> 00:21:36.490
But to sit down and go, all right,

461
00:21:36.490 --> 00:21:40.870
I'm gonna pour a lot of this dark stuff

462
00:21:40.870 --> 00:21:45.760
and inner demons and secrets

463
00:21:45.760 --> 00:21:48.270
out on the paper for the world to see,

464
00:21:48.270 --> 00:21:50.323
what was that emotionally like for you?

465
00:21:52.630 --> 00:21:55.623
In two words, it sucked.

466
00:21:58.956 --> 00:22:02.210
It was incredibly exhausting,

467
00:22:02.210 --> 00:22:05.093
and it was scary, and I dreaded it.

468
00:22:10.350 --> 00:22:13.150
There were a lot of times where I was like, ah,

469
00:22:13.150 --> 00:22:17.920
I gotta just not write anymore, or I can't stop writing.

470
00:22:17.920 --> 00:22:22.340
And often, I have both feelings at the same time.

471
00:22:22.340 --> 00:22:25.750
And this is the thing though,

472
00:22:25.750 --> 00:22:29.640
is that when I was writing that,

473
00:22:29.640 --> 00:22:32.160
I really had to go back and confront a lot of things

474
00:22:32.160 --> 00:22:36.940
that I'd put behind me or I felt that I'd put behind me,

475
00:22:36.940 --> 00:22:41.750
and it's like saying, okay,

476
00:22:41.750 --> 00:22:44.400
what are the two things I'm scared of the most?

477
00:22:44.400 --> 00:22:46.400
I'm scared of the dark and I'm scared of snakes.

478
00:22:46.400 --> 00:22:49.070
I'm gonna fill up, and I'm scared of drowning.

479
00:22:49.070 --> 00:22:51.100
I'm gonna fill a pool with snakes,

480
00:22:51.100 --> 00:22:53.000
turn out the lights, and jump into it.

481
00:22:54.429 --> 00:22:58.150
And so that sucked,

482
00:22:58.150 --> 00:23:03.150
but the end result of getting out of the pool alive

483
00:23:04.610 --> 00:23:08.920
and turning the lights back on, I was like,

484
00:23:08.920 --> 00:23:12.653
huh, even that didn't kill me.

485
00:23:13.617 --> 00:23:16.520
And then it's cathartic, and it's-

486
00:23:16.520 --> 00:23:19.010
Well, yeah, it's more than that.

487
00:23:19.010 --> 00:23:21.803
There's a sense of, yeah, there's a sense of,

488
00:23:22.640 --> 00:23:27.640
relief isn't the right word, but I guess freedom.

489
00:23:27.780 --> 00:23:30.580
Because just the simple act of writing it down

490
00:23:30.580 --> 00:23:33.510
on some level is making peace with it,

491
00:23:33.510 --> 00:23:35.193
or at least acknowledging it.

492
00:23:37.570 --> 00:23:42.020
Yeah, there's a lot of stuff in that story too

493
00:23:42.020 --> 00:23:44.370
that I was really reluctant to put out there

494
00:23:44.370 --> 00:23:49.370
because there were secrets.

495
00:23:49.870 --> 00:23:50.980
It was stuff that I'd hidden

496
00:23:50.980 --> 00:23:52.680
from my friends and my family,

497
00:23:52.680 --> 00:23:56.210
and with good reason.

498
00:23:56.210 --> 00:23:58.840
There's a lot of real dark,

499
00:23:58.840 --> 00:24:01.810
and raunchy, and horrible stuff in there.

500
00:24:01.810 --> 00:24:06.810
And most of it, sort of like deep, deep nihilism,

501
00:24:09.190 --> 00:24:14.190
and it's weird and alarming at times

502
00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:19.423
that that information is out there.

503
00:24:20.540 --> 00:24:22.040
But man, the response that I've gotten

504
00:24:22.040 --> 00:24:24.610
from people has just been so overwhelming.

505
00:24:24.610 --> 00:24:29.300
It's been, I've gotten so many emails

506
00:24:29.300 --> 00:24:32.017
and Facebook messages from people who are like,

507
00:24:32.017 --> 00:24:34.210
"Oh, this is just like my journey,"

508
00:24:34.210 --> 00:24:39.100
or "This was so meaningful to me."

509
00:24:39.100 --> 00:24:44.100
And also, I've had a bunch of people who don't run,

510
00:24:45.240 --> 00:24:48.980
who aren't athletic, who don't have substance abuse issues,

511
00:24:48.980 --> 00:24:52.790
who have read it and said, "Man, this really spoke to me."

512
00:24:52.790 --> 00:24:54.280
And-
It's weird, right?

513
00:24:54.280 --> 00:24:55.113
Yeah.

514
00:24:57.150 --> 00:25:02.150
And that makes me so happy that Dave pushed me to write it,

515
00:25:03.320 --> 00:25:08.320
and that I had the Chutzpah to go through with it.

516
00:25:10.550 --> 00:25:11.470
Well, they say in recovery

517
00:25:11.470 --> 00:25:13.880
that you're only as sick as your secrets.

518
00:25:13.880 --> 00:25:15.840
And when you hold onto those secrets

519
00:25:15.840 --> 00:25:18.340
and you harbor them and they fester,

520
00:25:18.340 --> 00:25:22.460
that you're only fertilizing them to grow larger.

521
00:25:22.460 --> 00:25:25.070
And when you expose them to the light,

522
00:25:25.070 --> 00:25:27.830
they perish, or they diminish.

523
00:25:27.830 --> 00:25:31.120
And so having the courage to do that.

524
00:25:31.120 --> 00:25:33.050
First of all, I applaud anyone

525
00:25:33.050 --> 00:25:35.040
who's willing to be that vulnerable.

526
00:25:35.040 --> 00:25:39.370
And I think that you're being authentic to who you are.

527
00:25:39.370 --> 00:25:43.380
And it takes a lot of courage to say, "This is who I am."

528
00:25:43.380 --> 00:25:47.090
And I'm staking my claim for that.

529
00:25:47.090 --> 00:25:50.570
And you're gonna think whatever you're gonna think about it.

530
00:25:50.570 --> 00:25:52.950
And for you to of be at peace with it enough

531
00:25:52.950 --> 00:25:55.519
to share that with the world is,

532
00:25:55.519 --> 00:25:58.363
it's a triumph for anybody to do that.

533
00:25:59.500 --> 00:26:01.928
Well, thanks, man, thanks.

534
00:26:01.928 --> 00:26:06.040
And I think that that's why

535
00:26:07.150 --> 00:26:08.700
this Single has done well,

536
00:26:08.700 --> 00:26:12.450
because it's authentic to who you are

537
00:26:12.450 --> 00:26:14.370
as a person and people see that.

538
00:26:14.370 --> 00:26:17.600
So even if they're not an athlete, they're not in recovery,

539
00:26:17.600 --> 00:26:19.420
they're not an addict or whatever,

540
00:26:19.420 --> 00:26:21.980
they can feel the authenticity,

541
00:26:21.980 --> 00:26:26.070
and that's what they're connecting to.

542
00:26:26.070 --> 00:26:31.070
Yeah, the way that I wrote it

543
00:26:31.530 --> 00:26:35.550
when I was writing it was I just tried to imagine

544
00:26:35.550 --> 00:26:38.542
that I was writing a long journal entry

545
00:26:38.542 --> 00:26:40.483
that nobody else would see.

546
00:26:42.922 --> 00:26:44.970
And to just be completely honest,

547
00:26:44.970 --> 00:26:48.667
as if I was speaking to a priest or something like that.

548
00:26:48.667 --> 00:26:49.990
And nobody would ever see it,

549
00:26:49.990 --> 00:26:51.963
nobody would ever hear it, no problem.

550
00:26:52.800 --> 00:26:54.810
So I just wrote with that in my mind.

551
00:26:54.810 --> 00:26:56.690
And then before I could stop myself,

552
00:26:56.690 --> 00:26:58.420
I sent it to my editor like that.

553
00:26:58.420 --> 00:27:00.320
And he was like, "This is great.

554
00:27:00.320 --> 00:27:01.940
I have a couple small changes and let's go."

555
00:27:01.940 --> 00:27:03.107
And was like, "Wait."

556
00:27:04.700 --> 00:27:05.533
That is,

557
00:27:05.533 --> 00:27:08.300
I'm laughing because that's exactly what I did.

558
00:27:08.300 --> 00:27:12.220
I would sit down and I'd start to write and I'd go,

559
00:27:12.220 --> 00:27:14.180
the only way that I can get this out is

560
00:27:14.180 --> 00:27:16.191
if I get in the mindset

561
00:27:16.191 --> 00:27:18.397
that nobody in the world is gonna read this.

562
00:27:18.397 --> 00:27:20.740
And I'm just writing it for myself.

563
00:27:20.740 --> 00:27:22.313
It's a private journal entry.

564
00:27:24.160 --> 00:27:25.450
But then every once in a while,

565
00:27:25.450 --> 00:27:28.630
I'd flash to some image in my mind

566
00:27:28.630 --> 00:27:30.940
of the book on a shelf at Barnes and Noble,

567
00:27:30.940 --> 00:27:33.003
and I would just absolutely panic,

568
00:27:34.749 --> 00:27:37.313
and be unable to write for a couple days.

569
00:27:37.313 --> 00:27:39.283
And I still remember.

570
00:27:40.566 --> 00:27:43.040
And I kind of overwrote everything,

571
00:27:43.040 --> 00:27:44.740
'cause I just wanted to get it all out.

572
00:27:44.740 --> 00:27:46.850
And then I'm like, now I have something to work with.

573
00:27:46.850 --> 00:27:48.510
And the manuscript that I turned in was

574
00:27:48.510 --> 00:27:50.070
like 150 pages longer

575
00:27:50.070 --> 00:27:52.200
than the book ultimately ended up being.

576
00:27:52.200 --> 00:27:57.200
And a lot of my drunkalog got cut down, a lot.

577
00:27:58.570 --> 00:28:01.350
And it was much darker and gnarlier.

578
00:28:01.350 --> 00:28:04.087
It got whitewashed a little bit by my editor.

579
00:28:04.087 --> 00:28:07.180
And I think, in the best interest of the book,

580
00:28:07.180 --> 00:28:08.710
and mine was a little bit different

581
00:28:08.710 --> 00:28:10.720
'cause I was trying to balance

582
00:28:10.720 --> 00:28:12.300
essentially three different stories.

583
00:28:12.300 --> 00:28:15.310
It's the addiction story, the athletic story,

584
00:28:15.310 --> 00:28:18.850
and then weaving a diet book in there at the same time.

585
00:28:18.850 --> 00:28:21.040
I was like, "How am I gonna make this all work?"

586
00:28:21.040 --> 00:28:24.910
And so a lot of stuff got trimmed.

587
00:28:24.910 --> 00:28:28.460
And I had some pretty dark episodes

588
00:28:28.460 --> 00:28:29.980
from the drinking days in there

589
00:28:29.980 --> 00:28:31.590
that ultimately didn't make it in.

590
00:28:31.590 --> 00:28:35.070
But I remember when I turned the manuscript into my editor,

591
00:28:35.070 --> 00:28:36.977
I just looked at my wife and I was like,

592
00:28:36.977 --> 00:28:39.700
"I hope this isn't the biggest mistake of my life."

593
00:28:39.700 --> 00:28:44.320
It's terrifying to be that exposed.

594
00:28:44.320 --> 00:28:45.790
Do I really wanna do this?

595
00:28:45.790 --> 00:28:48.520
Is this really a good idea or not?

596
00:28:48.520 --> 00:28:49.353
It's,

597
00:28:51.090 --> 00:28:56.090
before I was getting ready to publish "The Long Run,"

598
00:28:56.330 --> 00:28:58.060
I was speaking to a friend of mine, who's a writer,

599
00:28:58.060 --> 00:29:01.670
and I said, "I have grave misgivings

600
00:29:01.670 --> 00:29:03.100
about sending this out into the world,

601
00:29:03.100 --> 00:29:06.230
'cause it sort of exposes me as just sick,

602
00:29:06.230 --> 00:29:07.513
crappy human being.

603
00:29:11.099 --> 00:29:14.730
And not in a glamorous way, just like scumbag.

604
00:29:14.730 --> 00:29:17.407
And he looked at me and he was like,

605
00:29:17.407 --> 00:29:19.380
"Mishka, honestly,

606
00:29:19.380 --> 00:29:20.940
there's nothing you could publish now

607
00:29:20.940 --> 00:29:23.330
that's gonna make you seem worse

608
00:29:23.330 --> 00:29:25.630
than the stuff that you've already published."

609
00:29:28.892 --> 00:29:30.874
And I was like, "Great."

610
00:29:30.874 --> 00:29:34.440
So I guess, that is the thing though,

611
00:29:34.440 --> 00:29:39.440
is that once you come out with these stories,

612
00:29:41.436 --> 00:29:43.210
and you're like, "Oh yeah, I got really drunk.

613
00:29:43.210 --> 00:29:45.230
And then I pissed the bed,"

614
00:29:45.230 --> 00:29:50.230
then no one can embarrass you with it because you own it.

615
00:29:50.300 --> 00:29:51.440
'Cause that's your story.

616
00:29:51.440 --> 00:29:53.350
And you've told that story.

617
00:29:53.350 --> 00:29:56.680
And the other thing is that inevitably,

618
00:29:58.390 --> 00:30:00.100
if you tell that story,

619
00:30:00.100 --> 00:30:01.707
somebody will come up to you later and say,

620
00:30:01.707 --> 00:30:05.077
"Man, one time I pissed the bed too."

621
00:30:06.310 --> 00:30:08.493
And that person may be a really hot woman,

622
00:30:10.120 --> 00:30:13.160
Which actually, this leads me to my next question.

623
00:30:13.160 --> 00:30:15.300
And this has been like my burning question I wanna ask.

624
00:30:15.300 --> 00:30:17.150
Who's interviewing who here?

625
00:30:18.351 --> 00:30:21.190
We'll pass the hat back and forth.

626
00:30:21.190 --> 00:30:23.523
Does your wife Julie have a sister?

627
00:30:24.420 --> 00:30:27.480
Because when I was reading your book,

628
00:30:27.480 --> 00:30:31.160
I was like, "Man, this woman is

629
00:30:31.160 --> 00:30:35.080
like the patience of Gandhi or something."

630
00:30:35.080 --> 00:30:36.620
'Cause I know what drunks are like,

631
00:30:36.620 --> 00:30:38.970
and I know what it's like to live with a drunk.

632
00:30:40.699 --> 00:30:41.677
And not only that,

633
00:30:41.677 --> 00:30:43.360
and to live with somebody in recovery,

634
00:30:43.360 --> 00:30:44.193
to live with somebody

635
00:30:44.193 --> 00:30:45.980
who's trying to get back on their feet,

636
00:30:45.980 --> 00:30:48.050
and to live with somebody

637
00:30:48.050 --> 00:30:50.480
who's training as much as you were,

638
00:30:50.480 --> 00:30:52.760
and as hard as you were.

639
00:30:52.760 --> 00:30:56.800
And she really stuck with you

640
00:30:56.800 --> 00:30:58.840
through a radical transformation.

641
00:30:58.840 --> 00:30:59.990
Yeah, she did.

642
00:31:02.430 --> 00:31:03.263
First of all,

643
00:31:03.263 --> 00:31:06.850
she's much more spiritually evolved than I am for sure.

644
00:31:06.850 --> 00:31:09.913
And she walks her walk.

645
00:31:10.933 --> 00:31:13.600
We met after I got sober,

646
00:31:13.600 --> 00:31:18.233
so she didn't see me in the depths,

647
00:31:19.560 --> 00:31:24.560
but a lot of those behavior patterns crop up.

648
00:31:24.610 --> 00:31:29.330
And it becomes less about not drinking

649
00:31:29.330 --> 00:31:34.330
and it becomes more about acting out

650
00:31:34.680 --> 00:31:39.460
or behavior patterns that you see in alcoholics that are dry

651
00:31:39.460 --> 00:31:44.460
or not actively engaged in whatever works for them

652
00:31:44.650 --> 00:31:46.810
to keep them on the straight and narrow.

653
00:31:46.810 --> 00:31:48.750
And yeah, she's been amazing.

654
00:31:48.750 --> 00:31:51.350
And none of this, in all honesty,

655
00:31:51.350 --> 00:31:54.230
none of this would've happened, Ultraman, the book,

656
00:31:54.230 --> 00:31:55.700
everything that's come from it

657
00:31:55.700 --> 00:32:00.700
all starts with her patience and belief in me, and sort of,

658
00:32:04.365 --> 00:32:07.450
I guess what it is, initially,

659
00:32:07.450 --> 00:32:10.180
she was like, "You should try this, you should do that."

660
00:32:10.180 --> 00:32:12.960
She'd put books on my nightstand that I wouldn't read.

661
00:32:12.960 --> 00:32:15.220
She would say, "You should be trying this,"

662
00:32:15.220 --> 00:32:18.610
or "You need to change your diet, it's not looking good."

663
00:32:18.610 --> 00:32:20.982
She'd always like, she'd call me out.

664
00:32:20.982 --> 00:32:23.817
She's a strong person and she'd say,

665
00:32:23.817 --> 00:32:26.217
"You look like a hell, dude, what are you doing?

666
00:32:27.491 --> 00:32:29.970
And that wasn't working.

667
00:32:29.970 --> 00:32:33.280
She fatigued of that pattern with me.

668
00:32:33.280 --> 00:32:36.547
And she got to a place where she was like,

669
00:32:36.547 --> 00:32:38.950
"All right, if I'm gonna stay married to this guy,

670
00:32:38.950 --> 00:32:41.480
I'm gonna have to just love him the way he is

671
00:32:41.480 --> 00:32:46.020
and let go of my desire or need for him to change.

672
00:32:46.020 --> 00:32:50.050
Even if I can see through this mirage of who he is,

673
00:32:50.050 --> 00:32:52.510
and I can see a better version of him,

674
00:32:52.510 --> 00:32:54.350
and I can see how he can get there,

675
00:32:54.350 --> 00:32:55.760
but he's not listening to me.

676
00:32:55.760 --> 00:32:58.280
And I'm just banging my head against the wall.

677
00:32:58.280 --> 00:33:01.230
And it was when she finally let go

678
00:33:01.230 --> 00:33:05.920
and detach from that completely that I began to change.

679
00:33:05.920 --> 00:33:10.280
And so there's this weird spiritual calculus

680
00:33:10.280 --> 00:33:11.300
that took place in that.

681
00:33:11.300 --> 00:33:14.607
And maybe on some unconscious level, I was registering like,

682
00:33:14.607 --> 00:33:16.490
"Oh, wait, she's not pushing me anymore.

683
00:33:16.490 --> 00:33:17.657
Now it's on me."

684
00:33:18.588 --> 00:33:20.637
So it wouldn't be like I'm doing it for her.

685
00:33:20.637 --> 00:33:24.270
I have to own this situation that I'm in.

686
00:33:24.270 --> 00:33:26.260
I have to own my behavior patterns

687
00:33:26.260 --> 00:33:29.460
and I have to wanna change for myself.

688
00:33:29.460 --> 00:33:31.450
And honestly, you can't sustain

689
00:33:31.450 --> 00:33:33.150
any kind of lifestyle change anyway

690
00:33:33.150 --> 00:33:35.643
unless you inherently want it inside of you.

691
00:33:36.610 --> 00:33:39.223
Yeah, that's the thing is you can't,

692
00:33:40.210 --> 00:33:43.630
it's incredibly difficult to force someone to change.

693
00:33:43.630 --> 00:33:48.630
And even if you force them to change, it's not gonna stick.

694
00:33:48.970 --> 00:33:49.850
No, of course not,

695
00:33:49.850 --> 00:33:51.500
'cause they're not doing it for the right reasons.

696
00:33:51.500 --> 00:33:54.250
They're doing it to please somebody else rather than,

697
00:33:54.250 --> 00:33:55.980
they're not internally driven to do it.

698
00:33:55.980 --> 00:33:57.740
Like, "Oh, I don't want my wife to leave me,

699
00:33:57.740 --> 00:33:58.590
so I'm gonna change."

700
00:33:58.590 --> 00:34:01.670
Well, although I understand that,

701
00:34:01.670 --> 00:34:04.070
and it's not a bad reason,

702
00:34:04.070 --> 00:34:06.100
but the chances of that

703
00:34:06.100 --> 00:34:09.200
being a sustainable change are not good.

704
00:34:09.200 --> 00:34:12.840
You can't force someone to want to change.

705
00:34:12.840 --> 00:34:15.917
And I think that she recognized that.

706
00:34:15.917 --> 00:34:17.113
It's funny.

707
00:34:17.993 --> 00:34:22.010
It's a very big, very gracious gesture

708
00:34:22.010 --> 00:34:23.904
that you make by saying that none of this

709
00:34:23.904 --> 00:34:25.370
would have happened without her.

710
00:34:25.370 --> 00:34:28.580
But I think that it speaks to your strength as a writer,

711
00:34:28.580 --> 00:34:31.410
because that's totally transparent in the book,

712
00:34:31.410 --> 00:34:35.150
and you make it abundantly clear to readers

713
00:34:35.150 --> 00:34:38.763
that this is a group effort.

714
00:34:38.763 --> 00:34:41.080
That she really had your back at every step.

715
00:34:41.080 --> 00:34:43.360
Yeah, and I acknowledge the support.

716
00:34:43.360 --> 00:34:45.710
Left to my own devices,

717
00:34:45.710 --> 00:34:48.070
I'm pissing the bed and eating Jack in the Box

718
00:34:48.070 --> 00:34:53.070
and living like a Hobbit in 10-day-old underwear.

719
00:34:54.900 --> 00:34:59.900
It's like, I'm not good on my own, and I don't trust myself.

720
00:35:00.660 --> 00:35:01.630
What I'm meant to say is,

721
00:35:01.630 --> 00:35:04.290
I have no idea what you're talking about, Rich.

722
00:35:04.290 --> 00:35:05.940
I've never been like that.

723
00:35:05.940 --> 00:35:08.590
I don't, yeah. (laughs)

724
00:35:08.590 --> 00:35:10.700
But no, it's true.

725
00:35:10.700 --> 00:35:15.700
And she actually wrote a blog post on that very subject

726
00:35:16.520 --> 00:35:18.850
that was on mind, body, green that did really well,

727
00:35:18.850 --> 00:35:21.770
kind of navigating relationships

728
00:35:21.770 --> 00:35:23.130
and pitfalls in relationships.

729
00:35:23.130 --> 00:35:25.840
And how she kind of approaches me when I'm difficult,

730
00:35:25.840 --> 00:35:29.160
which is on a regular basis.

731
00:35:29.160 --> 00:35:33.300
And her ability to just be nonplussed

732
00:35:33.300 --> 00:35:35.427
by whatever I'm doing, and kind of like,

733
00:35:35.427 --> 00:35:37.298
"Hey man, that's you.

734
00:35:37.298 --> 00:35:40.350
Don't invade my space

735
00:35:40.350 --> 00:35:43.790
with whatever nonsense that you're coming at me with,

736
00:35:43.790 --> 00:35:46.720
whether it's negative energy or whatever it is."

737
00:35:46.720 --> 00:35:50.830
And it takes a strong constitution for that.

738
00:35:50.830 --> 00:35:52.020
Yeah, yeah.

739
00:35:52.020 --> 00:35:53.020
And so I'm lucky.

740
00:35:53.020 --> 00:35:55.230
I'm blessed in that regard.

741
00:35:55.230 --> 00:35:58.460
And I realize not everybody is in a relationship like that,

742
00:35:58.460 --> 00:36:00.163
and I don't take it for granted.

743
00:36:01.250 --> 00:36:05.800
I, more than one friend has commented to me

744
00:36:05.800 --> 00:36:09.890
that I'm an angry guy and that I get angry easily,

745
00:36:09.890 --> 00:36:13.123
and that when I get angry, it's explosive.

746
00:36:14.026 --> 00:36:17.520
And that's one of the things that,

747
00:36:17.520 --> 00:36:19.400
that's one of the things about getting sober that's tough,

748
00:36:19.400 --> 00:36:24.400
man, is because back in the day, when I got mad,

749
00:36:27.370 --> 00:36:29.330
I'd have a drink and I'd feel better.

750
00:36:29.330 --> 00:36:31.960
Or when I got sad, I'd have a drink and I'd feel better.

751
00:36:31.960 --> 00:36:35.240
Or when I couldn't get to sleep,

752
00:36:35.240 --> 00:36:37.050
I'd have a couple of drinks, then I could sleep.

753
00:36:37.050 --> 00:36:38.320
And when I couldn't wake up,

754
00:36:38.320 --> 00:36:41.212
I'd have a drink, and that would wake me up.

755
00:36:41.212 --> 00:36:43.400
And then I got to the point where I was taking

756
00:36:43.400 --> 00:36:45.350
all these different pills and stuff too.

757
00:36:45.350 --> 00:36:47.791
Oh, I'm too stressed out, I'm gonna take a Xanax.

758
00:36:47.791 --> 00:36:50.490
I need to do a line

759
00:36:50.490 --> 00:36:52.240
in order to get through the show or whatever.

760
00:36:52.240 --> 00:36:55.940
And then when you get sober, you're left with nothing.

761
00:36:55.940 --> 00:36:57.830
Right, all your medicine is taken away.

762
00:36:57.830 --> 00:36:59.820
You used all these substances

763
00:36:59.820 --> 00:37:02.463
to manage your emotional being.

764
00:37:03.971 --> 00:37:04.840
It worked for a while,

765
00:37:04.840 --> 00:37:07.540
or obviously you wouldn't have done it as long as you did.

766
00:37:07.540 --> 00:37:09.360
And then it stops working.

767
00:37:09.360 --> 00:37:11.893
And then you're faced with this dilemma

768
00:37:11.893 --> 00:37:14.240
and this letting go process.

769
00:37:14.240 --> 00:37:17.710
And then you have that momentary elation of being sober,

770
00:37:17.710 --> 00:37:20.480
and the great accomplishment that that is.

771
00:37:20.480 --> 00:37:23.980
And then that wears off, and then you're like, "Now what?"

772
00:37:23.980 --> 00:37:26.140
You're, like we said before,

773
00:37:26.140 --> 00:37:28.530
a raw nerve without the tools

774
00:37:28.530 --> 00:37:32.160
to deal with those emotions when they come up.

775
00:37:32.160 --> 00:37:35.900
And I think that it gets into a little bit, for me,

776
00:37:35.900 --> 00:37:39.690
and without breaking anonymity or whatever,

777
00:37:39.690 --> 00:37:43.383
I work a certain program that works for me.

778
00:37:44.300 --> 00:37:45.571
And I know that you have

779
00:37:45.571 --> 00:37:48.520
a different approach to your sobriety.

780
00:37:48.520 --> 00:37:51.090
But I know that if I'm not using the tools

781
00:37:51.090 --> 00:37:56.090
that I learn and that I utilize in the rooms,

782
00:37:56.420 --> 00:37:59.290
as they say, I'm in trouble.

783
00:37:59.290 --> 00:38:04.290
Yeah, and listen, it's funny.

784
00:38:04.402 --> 00:38:07.590
When you do something that people take attention to,

785
00:38:07.590 --> 00:38:09.460
you often become the poster boy

786
00:38:09.460 --> 00:38:11.060
for several different causes,

787
00:38:11.060 --> 00:38:13.770
some of which you don't support,

788
00:38:13.770 --> 00:38:17.043
or you're uneasy with the amount of support,

789
00:38:18.220 --> 00:38:20.320
you're uneasy in the way you've been cast.

790
00:38:22.490 --> 00:38:25.253
Yeah, I quit, I stopped drinking on my own.

791
00:38:26.550 --> 00:38:29.340
After I had been quit for a little while,

792
00:38:29.340 --> 00:38:30.720
I started going to talk therapy,

793
00:38:30.720 --> 00:38:34.660
and that was helpful for a little while.

794
00:38:34.660 --> 00:38:38.070
The counselor I was talking to wasn't incredibly helpful.

795
00:38:38.070 --> 00:38:39.730
And then I switched and started talking

796
00:38:39.730 --> 00:38:41.870
to another counselor and that was better,

797
00:38:41.870 --> 00:38:46.870
but I didn't go through any peer network,

798
00:38:49.610 --> 00:38:54.083
or I didn't go to any group or group therapy or anything.

799
00:38:55.474 --> 00:38:56.850
And so a lot of people look to me

800
00:38:56.850 --> 00:39:00.930
as the guy who's going to puncture

801
00:39:00.930 --> 00:39:03.520
this horrible organization that is AA.

802
00:39:03.520 --> 00:39:05.913
And that's not how I feel.

803
00:39:07.607 --> 00:39:11.850
I have a lot of friends in the program,

804
00:39:11.850 --> 00:39:15.103
and I think there's a lot of wisdom that's come out of it.

805
00:39:16.770 --> 00:39:21.770
Me, who I am is sort of pigheaded guy that I am,

806
00:39:22.050 --> 00:39:23.773
I gotta do it my way, man.

807
00:39:24.720 --> 00:39:27.870
And that's the only way it was gonna work for me.

808
00:39:27.870 --> 00:39:32.870
If I had to, if somebody, if I had to get sober in jail,

809
00:39:32.970 --> 00:39:36.150
then it would've lasted until I got out.

810
00:39:36.150 --> 00:39:41.150
And if I, especially if in the early days,

811
00:39:42.400 --> 00:39:46.890
I had people telling me, you've gotta do it a certain way,

812
00:39:46.890 --> 00:39:50.510
you've gotta do it a certain way, I would've just,

813
00:39:50.510 --> 00:39:53.320
that would've driven me right back to it.

814
00:39:53.320 --> 00:39:57.720
Yeah, and I'm not here to judge you in any respect.

815
00:39:57.720 --> 00:39:59.620
You've stayed sober for four years

816
00:39:59.620 --> 00:40:02.200
and you found a way that works for you.

817
00:40:02.200 --> 00:40:07.200
And I have no opinion on that.

818
00:40:07.210 --> 00:40:08.803
I just know for myself,

819
00:40:09.740 --> 00:40:12.033
my way is usually not the right way.

820
00:40:13.310 --> 00:40:17.910
And I tried my way for many years.

821
00:40:17.910 --> 00:40:20.238
I didn't wanna come into AA.

822
00:40:20.238 --> 00:40:21.071
I wanted to do it myself.

823
00:40:21.071 --> 00:40:23.620
And I had my ideas about how I could solve the problem.

824
00:40:23.620 --> 00:40:25.940
And none of those worked, and the hole just got deeper.

825
00:40:25.940 --> 00:40:29.350
And it wasn't until I got to a place

826
00:40:29.350 --> 00:40:33.120
where I was able to quash my ego and say,

827
00:40:33.120 --> 00:40:34.560
well, maybe there's a better way,

828
00:40:34.560 --> 00:40:37.470
or set aside my notion of what I thought was best.

829
00:40:37.470 --> 00:40:42.470
And really, not try to will myself into the solution,

830
00:40:43.350 --> 00:40:46.330
but to surrender and allow other people

831
00:40:46.330 --> 00:40:47.890
to help me and to take direction

832
00:40:47.890 --> 00:40:49.840
and to kind of have that humility,

833
00:40:49.840 --> 00:40:53.000
which is not my default setting

834
00:40:53.000 --> 00:40:54.973
by any stretch of the imagination,

835
00:40:56.100 --> 00:41:00.880
was when I allowed this solution to take root.

836
00:41:00.880 --> 00:41:02.000
And that's what worked.

837
00:41:02.000 --> 00:41:05.240
That's what's worked for me and continues to work for me.

838
00:41:05.240 --> 00:41:07.113
Yeah, it's funny,

839
00:41:08.350 --> 00:41:11.670
what you said about having no judgment.

840
00:41:11.670 --> 00:41:15.160
That's exactly the same way that I feel towards you.

841
00:41:15.160 --> 00:41:19.550
Actually, no, it's deeper than that,

842
00:41:19.550 --> 00:41:22.720
which is that I support you

843
00:41:22.720 --> 00:41:26.940
no matter what way you're using to make your life better.

844
00:41:26.940 --> 00:41:31.940
And that's the thing is that if someone gets sober

845
00:41:32.930 --> 00:41:37.050
by doing jigsaw puzzles four hours a day,

846
00:41:37.050 --> 00:41:42.050
and that's their thing, I'm for it, I support it.

847
00:41:42.466 --> 00:41:47.466
Whatever process you need to feel better,

848
00:41:48.830 --> 00:41:51.700
to live better, to have a more fulfilling life,

849
00:41:51.700 --> 00:41:54.644
whatever works for you, I totally support.

850
00:41:54.644 --> 00:41:59.190
And one of the most interesting stories

851
00:41:59.190 --> 00:42:03.470
that I've run into after the publication

852
00:42:03.470 --> 00:42:08.470
of "The Long Run" is my friend Gerard in Portland.

853
00:42:08.600 --> 00:42:11.972
And I've met him a couple of times,

854
00:42:11.972 --> 00:42:14.180
when I was like on tour and stuff like that.

855
00:42:14.180 --> 00:42:16.960
Big boy, over 400 pounds.

856
00:42:16.960 --> 00:42:20.930
I think he's lost 80 pounds to date,

857
00:42:20.930 --> 00:42:24.230
which just blows my mind.

858
00:42:24.230 --> 00:42:29.230
It makes me think that what I did of stopping drinking

859
00:42:29.750 --> 00:42:33.540
and running a couple of 50 mile races is nothing.

860
00:42:33.540 --> 00:42:38.540
Because when, and I think that's one of the things

861
00:42:38.970 --> 00:42:43.970
that you and I are fortunate in, being primarily drunks,

862
00:42:45.420 --> 00:42:50.420
is that you don't need alcohol from day to day to live.

863
00:42:50.550 --> 00:42:52.830
But you need food.
But you need food.

864
00:42:52.830 --> 00:42:57.820
Yeah, and food is, I look at,

865
00:42:57.820 --> 00:42:59.650
I tend, because of my experience,

866
00:42:59.650 --> 00:43:01.520
I tend to look at a lot of things

867
00:43:01.520 --> 00:43:04.700
through the prism of addiction and recovery.

868
00:43:04.700 --> 00:43:09.700
And the longer I walk this path,

869
00:43:09.780 --> 00:43:14.780
the more I'm convinced that it transcends alcohol and drugs,

870
00:43:17.410 --> 00:43:22.410
and it invades all of our behavior patterns,

871
00:43:22.880 --> 00:43:26.800
and our relationship to television or food,

872
00:43:26.800 --> 00:43:30.050
and foods that don't service, or exercise,

873
00:43:30.050 --> 00:43:33.980
or gambling, or porn, or whatever it is,

874
00:43:33.980 --> 00:43:37.423
whatever your vice is or your secret is or whatever.

875
00:43:38.310 --> 00:43:40.900
I think that it's rampant.

876
00:43:40.900 --> 00:43:43.770
And I think that it's like,

877
00:43:43.770 --> 00:43:46.670
the analogy is like in the '70s,

878
00:43:46.670 --> 00:43:50.350
when they called smoking a habit, and nobody,

879
00:43:50.350 --> 00:43:53.460
the idea that nicotine was addicting was

880
00:43:53.460 --> 00:43:56.030
not even part of the discourse.

881
00:43:56.030 --> 00:43:59.720
And it's like, oh, I have this habit of watching reality TV,

882
00:43:59.720 --> 00:44:02.970
or I have this habit of doing this or that.

883
00:44:02.970 --> 00:44:06.300
And it's like, no, actually, you need to look at it like,

884
00:44:06.300 --> 00:44:07.300
these things really are,

885
00:44:07.300 --> 00:44:10.240
just because they're behaviors and not substances,

886
00:44:10.240 --> 00:44:13.300
they have equal power over us.

887
00:44:13.300 --> 00:44:15.610
And somebody who, I have a friend

888
00:44:15.610 --> 00:44:18.987
who has been sober a long time.

889
00:44:18.987 --> 00:44:20.623
He's been sober 21 years.

890
00:44:22.120 --> 00:44:25.270
Amazing guy, former heroin addict.

891
00:44:25.270 --> 00:44:26.103
And he's like,

892
00:44:27.160 --> 00:44:29.330
I never thought that anything

893
00:44:29.330 --> 00:44:30.840
would be harder than kicking heroin,

894
00:44:30.840 --> 00:44:33.350
but I'm having such a hard time with food.

895
00:44:33.350 --> 00:44:35.560
I just can't solve this problem.

896
00:44:35.560 --> 00:44:39.180
And he's finally starting to really realize

897
00:44:39.180 --> 00:44:43.440
how his relationship with food is addiction-based.

898
00:44:43.440 --> 00:44:44.860
And breaking those patterns

899
00:44:44.860 --> 00:44:47.410
because you do have to eat every day

900
00:44:47.410 --> 00:44:49.660
can make your life really unmanageable,

901
00:44:49.660 --> 00:44:52.003
and the health consequences are profound.

902
00:44:52.870 --> 00:44:57.623
Yeah, it's ironic.

903
00:44:58.500 --> 00:44:59.430
For the last couple of months,

904
00:44:59.430 --> 00:45:03.130
I've been working with my buddy Jed Collins who works,

905
00:45:03.130 --> 00:45:07.193
who lives downstairs, who's a, he's a comic artist.

906
00:45:08.637 --> 00:45:10.650
So we're putting together a comic book

907
00:45:10.650 --> 00:45:15.650
from a story that I wrote 10 years ago, 15 years ago?

908
00:45:17.214 --> 00:45:19.010
And it basically follows,

909
00:45:19.010 --> 00:45:20.950
there's one episode when I was a little kid

910
00:45:20.950 --> 00:45:24.900
where I stole a big bag of chocolate chips.

911
00:45:24.900 --> 00:45:29.900
And then my mom busted me with them downstairs,

912
00:45:31.040 --> 00:45:33.477
just sort of like chocolate all over my face.

913
00:45:33.477 --> 00:45:34.790
It was the summertime.

914
00:45:34.790 --> 00:45:36.410
And I think I was like,

915
00:45:36.410 --> 00:45:37.700
I think the only thing that I had

916
00:45:37.700 --> 00:45:39.520
that had pockets was my winter coat,

917
00:45:39.520 --> 00:45:41.880
so I put my winter coat on.

918
00:45:41.880 --> 00:45:43.780
So I'm way too hot in my winter coat

919
00:45:43.780 --> 00:45:45.660
with the chocolate chips in my pocket,

920
00:45:45.660 --> 00:45:46.940
chocolate all over my hands

921
00:45:46.940 --> 00:45:49.090
and my fingers and all over my face.

922
00:45:49.090 --> 00:45:54.090
And she just had this response when she caught me,

923
00:45:54.960 --> 00:45:59.340
she was like, "Oh no, what's coming?"

924
00:45:59.340 --> 00:46:02.840
And I think she knew then that I was gonna wind up

925
00:46:02.840 --> 00:46:05.225
to have big problems down the line.

926
00:46:05.225 --> 00:46:07.095
These kind of issue.

927
00:46:07.095 --> 00:46:12.095
And I still, I have a friend

928
00:46:13.960 --> 00:46:17.140
who I just love to death who's in the program.

929
00:46:17.140 --> 00:46:20.660
And he and I used to be drinking buddies.

930
00:46:20.660 --> 00:46:24.473
And he actually taught me how to drink.

931
00:46:25.590 --> 00:46:29.090
And to watch him eat is like,

932
00:46:31.690 --> 00:46:32.870
it brings me right back

933
00:46:32.870 --> 00:46:34.983
to when we would go out drinking together,

934
00:46:36.110 --> 00:46:40.360
and just be like, bring me four Jack and Cokes.

935
00:46:40.360 --> 00:46:45.360
And to see him stirring a big thing

936
00:46:45.600 --> 00:46:47.800
of ice cream with a Snicker's bar

937
00:46:47.800 --> 00:46:51.329
and putting honey on the ice cream and stirring.

938
00:46:51.329 --> 00:46:54.910
That's an unhealthy relationship

939
00:46:54.910 --> 00:46:55.970
with food right there.

940
00:46:55.970 --> 00:46:59.630
And when you're an addict or an alcoholic

941
00:46:59.630 --> 00:47:01.320
and you remove the substances, again,

942
00:47:01.320 --> 00:47:03.720
it's like the behavior patterns are still there.

943
00:47:04.650 --> 00:47:08.160
There's work that needs to be done to modify that.

944
00:47:08.160 --> 00:47:10.070
When I was talking about that,

945
00:47:10.070 --> 00:47:11.630
my mouth started watering.

946
00:47:11.630 --> 00:47:14.710
'Cause I was like, oh shit, that sounds really good.

947
00:47:14.710 --> 00:47:18.523
Yeah, it's powerful for sure.

948
00:47:19.550 --> 00:47:22.050
And when you were talking about your anger issues,

949
00:47:24.790 --> 00:47:28.980
what are the tools that you use to quell that, or manage it,

950
00:47:28.980 --> 00:47:32.220
or what is your plan for overcoming that

951
00:47:32.220 --> 00:47:34.950
or transcending this behavior pattern

952
00:47:34.950 --> 00:47:37.833
that isn't serving you, or is causing you,

953
00:47:38.910 --> 00:47:40.593
is disruptive in your life?

954
00:47:42.130 --> 00:47:45.480
Rich, I'm starting to figure it out, man.

955
00:47:45.480 --> 00:47:46.700
I'm starting to grow up.

956
00:47:46.700 --> 00:47:51.380
I think I've grown up from 13 to maybe 15 now.

957
00:47:51.380 --> 00:47:53.683
And I'm proud of myself for that.

958
00:47:56.810 --> 00:48:01.810
A big thing is it's not unfamiliar for me anymore.

959
00:48:04.140 --> 00:48:06.950
So now when something happens and I get angry,

960
00:48:06.950 --> 00:48:10.730
it's like, oh, I'm ready for this.

961
00:48:10.730 --> 00:48:15.120
It's the enemy I know versus the enemy I don't know.

962
00:48:15.120 --> 00:48:19.720
And when I got really mad before,

963
00:48:19.720 --> 00:48:22.400
I would just go out and run until I couldn't run anymore,

964
00:48:22.400 --> 00:48:25.210
do pushups until I couldn't do pushups anymore.

965
00:48:25.210 --> 00:48:27.540
And I think those are fine outlets,

966
00:48:27.540 --> 00:48:30.257
because that's a great way to get motivated

967
00:48:32.059 --> 00:48:33.380
for a workout, but-
Of course.

968
00:48:33.380 --> 00:48:34.540
It's not always-
It's better

969
00:48:34.540 --> 00:48:37.490
than picking up a drink or popping a pill.

970
00:48:37.490 --> 00:48:39.860
But I think the,

971
00:48:39.860 --> 00:48:42.780
and I think this is a part that the place

972
00:48:42.780 --> 00:48:44.960
in our lives that we're both in now is

973
00:48:44.960 --> 00:48:47.490
we're trying to narrow the aperture,

974
00:48:47.490 --> 00:48:52.147
so you get alcohol out of your life, and that's a huge step.

975
00:48:52.147 --> 00:48:53.890
And then it's breaking that food addiction,

976
00:48:53.890 --> 00:48:55.013
that's another huge step.

977
00:48:55.013 --> 00:48:56.633
And you just sort of like,

978
00:48:58.690 --> 00:49:00.910
each day or each month or each year,

979
00:49:00.910 --> 00:49:03.020
just get a little bit better.

980
00:49:03.020 --> 00:49:04.280
Yeah, absolutely.

981
00:49:04.280 --> 00:49:06.770
They say the road gets narrower, you know what I mean?

982
00:49:06.770 --> 00:49:08.190
Because certain things

983
00:49:08.190 --> 00:49:11.530
that used to not bother you in your behavior,

984
00:49:11.530 --> 00:49:13.323
or I should speak for myself,

985
00:49:14.240 --> 00:49:16.650
then suddenly they become bothersome to me.

986
00:49:16.650 --> 00:49:17.990
You know what?

987
00:49:17.990 --> 00:49:20.550
It's no longer acceptable to me that I do this,

988
00:49:20.550 --> 00:49:21.383
whatever it is.

989
00:49:21.383 --> 00:49:23.280
And then it's time to look at that pattern.

990
00:49:23.280 --> 00:49:26.840
But it's also like squeezing a water balloon.

991
00:49:26.840 --> 00:49:28.980
Once you feel like you've pushed down

992
00:49:28.980 --> 00:49:30.910
and gotten a hold on one kind of thing,

993
00:49:30.910 --> 00:49:33.000
then it pops out somewhere else.

994
00:49:33.000 --> 00:49:36.050
And so there's no destination.

995
00:49:36.050 --> 00:49:40.210
It's a life journey that will never be fully mastered

996
00:49:40.210 --> 00:49:42.140
by any stretch of the imagination.

997
00:49:42.140 --> 00:49:45.960
So it's just about what is the progress that you can make,

998
00:49:45.960 --> 00:49:47.870
and where are the improvements?

999
00:49:47.870 --> 00:49:48.920
Where do I need to be?

1000
00:49:48.920 --> 00:49:50.790
What do I need to be working on next

1001
00:49:50.790 --> 00:49:54.640
that is the next frontier.

1002
00:49:54.640 --> 00:49:59.640
Yeah, and it's like that annoying mantra

1003
00:49:59.990 --> 00:50:02.260
that your buddy kept throwing in your face.

1004
00:50:02.260 --> 00:50:04.060
That's why they call it a challenge.

1005
00:50:05.970 --> 00:50:09.760
It's hard, but it's hard because it's supposed to be hard.

1006
00:50:09.760 --> 00:50:12.650
If it was easy, it wouldn't be worthwhile.

1007
00:50:12.650 --> 00:50:15.090
And am I gonna be fighting this fight

1008
00:50:15.090 --> 00:50:16.080
for the rest of my life?

1009
00:50:16.080 --> 00:50:16.950
Yeah.

1010
00:50:16.950 --> 00:50:18.100
Am I up for it?

1011
00:50:18.100 --> 00:50:19.510
Hell yeah.

1012
00:50:19.510 --> 00:50:21.193
It's worth doing, man.

1013
00:50:23.520 --> 00:50:25.633
I really just went from,

1014
00:50:27.800 --> 00:50:30.470
I couldn't even hold down a job in a bar.

1015
00:50:30.470 --> 00:50:32.570
And the guys at the bars were

1016
00:50:32.570 --> 00:50:34.950
always so nice when they fired me,

1017
00:50:34.950 --> 00:50:36.210
or usually they were,

1018
00:50:36.210 --> 00:50:40.950
and it was just the shrug and the smile.

1019
00:50:40.950 --> 00:50:42.400
What he wants to do.

1020
00:50:43.480 --> 00:50:45.923
You know and I know what you're doing.

1021
00:50:46.830 --> 00:50:48.429
Yeah.

1022
00:50:48.429 --> 00:50:49.410
And it's that thing,

1023
00:50:49.410 --> 00:50:50.850
like, here's something I wanted to get,

1024
00:50:50.850 --> 00:50:52.540
I didn't mean to interrupt you.

1025
00:50:52.540 --> 00:50:54.370
Finish your thought.

1026
00:50:54.370 --> 00:50:56.697
I had one boss once who said,

1027
00:50:56.697 --> 00:50:58.767
"We just feel like you don't care."

1028
00:51:00.152 --> 00:51:02.530
I was like, bingo, yeah.

1029
00:51:02.530 --> 00:51:04.149
Yeah, you tend not to care

1030
00:51:04.149 --> 00:51:05.890
when you're drinking a lot,

1031
00:51:05.890 --> 00:51:08.636
but there's this thing that I got,

1032
00:51:08.636 --> 00:51:11.130
that I'm getting from you that I relate to a lot,

1033
00:51:11.130 --> 00:51:14.530
which is this idea of, on the one hand,

1034
00:51:14.530 --> 00:51:16.940
when you're out there and you're using,

1035
00:51:16.940 --> 00:51:18.640
there's that deep shame,

1036
00:51:18.640 --> 00:51:20.210
'cause you're harboring this secret,

1037
00:51:20.210 --> 00:51:23.130
and it's just, it's horrible.

1038
00:51:23.130 --> 00:51:24.710
You don't want anyone to know,

1039
00:51:24.710 --> 00:51:28.433
and you just feel like the lowest person on earth.

1040
00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:33.300
And yet at the same time simultaneously,

1041
00:51:33.300 --> 00:51:38.300
while you're letting that fester,

1042
00:51:39.150 --> 00:51:41.460
this idea that you're better than everyone else.

1043
00:51:41.460 --> 00:51:46.460
This superiority complex and in inferiority complex

1044
00:51:46.570 --> 00:51:50.703
operating at absolutely the same instant.

1045
00:51:52.210 --> 00:51:53.320
It's funny.

1046
00:51:53.320 --> 00:51:57.470
I called a really good friend out on that the other day.

1047
00:51:57.470 --> 00:52:00.690
And when I was driving home, I was like,

1048
00:52:00.690 --> 00:52:03.170
how was I able to recognize that in him?

1049
00:52:03.170 --> 00:52:07.363
And then I was like, oh yeah, 'cause that's what I do.

1050
00:52:08.520 --> 00:52:13.520
And yeah, I definitely had

1051
00:52:13.981 --> 00:52:18.981
that combination of arrogance and insecurity,

1052
00:52:19.820 --> 00:52:24.493
where I was like, I'm the worst thing ever.

1053
00:52:25.540 --> 00:52:27.913
I have no merit whatsoever.

1054
00:52:30.040 --> 00:52:35.040
But if I'm an drunk and I can still do the stuff that I do,

1055
00:52:35.430 --> 00:52:37.790
and you guys aren't drunk and you can't do it,

1056
00:52:37.790 --> 00:52:41.560
then you're more pathetic than I am.

1057
00:52:41.560 --> 00:52:44.503
And that's a horrible way to live.

1058
00:52:45.520 --> 00:52:49.747
It's a weird, false pride that I had as well.

1059
00:52:52.380 --> 00:52:55.860
And I think our stories are different in many ways,

1060
00:52:55.860 --> 00:52:58.830
but the important aspects of our story,

1061
00:52:58.830 --> 00:52:59.830
I think, are very common.

1062
00:52:59.830 --> 00:53:04.830
And that is that idea of, like, hey man, I can go out,

1063
00:53:05.620 --> 00:53:09.880
and I can do Jager shots all night and drink 25 beers,

1064
00:53:09.880 --> 00:53:12.870
and I'm still gonna be better

1065
00:53:12.870 --> 00:53:14.930
than you at this test in college,

1066
00:53:14.930 --> 00:53:16.730
or write better paper than you,

1067
00:53:16.730 --> 00:53:20.530
and get the A or whatever, get into Columbia,

1068
00:53:20.530 --> 00:53:25.180
all these sorts of things that, they keep you out there,

1069
00:53:25.180 --> 00:53:27.050
because you think that you can keep,

1070
00:53:27.050 --> 00:53:29.780
you can do this and you can do it better than anyone else.

1071
00:53:29.780 --> 00:53:33.730
And burning it at both, burning the candle at both ends.

1072
00:53:33.730 --> 00:53:38.730
Yeah, and academics are a great example of that,

1073
00:53:39.540 --> 00:53:44.540
because it's only in the last six months, maybe,

1074
00:53:46.720 --> 00:53:51.720
that I'm starting to realize that I'm gifted at writing.

1075
00:53:54.290 --> 00:53:55.340
And you are.

1076
00:53:57.150 --> 00:53:58.840
Now I'm figuring out

1077
00:53:58.840 --> 00:54:02.130
that it just comes easier to me

1078
00:54:02.130 --> 00:54:03.560
than it does to other people.

1079
00:54:03.560 --> 00:54:06.160
Because I watch some of my musician friends

1080
00:54:06.160 --> 00:54:07.950
who will just hear a song on the radio

1081
00:54:07.950 --> 00:54:09.600
and just pick up a guitar and figure it out,

1082
00:54:09.600 --> 00:54:12.080
or like they're playing it lefty,

1083
00:54:12.080 --> 00:54:12.913
they're playing it righty,

1084
00:54:12.913 --> 00:54:14.530
and they can just figure,

1085
00:54:14.530 --> 00:54:16.730
learn new instruments no problem.

1086
00:54:16.730 --> 00:54:20.510
And I had labor at playing guitar.

1087
00:54:20.510 --> 00:54:25.510
I've been playing for 30 years, and I'm the worst, and-

1088
00:54:25.669 --> 00:54:29.243
Yeah, but you're also, you're not the worst, dude.

1089
00:54:30.400 --> 00:54:32.350
Here, you're in these bands,

1090
00:54:32.350 --> 00:54:33.830
and you've played with all these people

1091
00:54:33.830 --> 00:54:35.550
and you've toured and you've got albums

1092
00:54:35.550 --> 00:54:36.820
and you've got apartment here

1093
00:54:36.820 --> 00:54:39.340
that's got like 7,000 guitars in it,

1094
00:54:39.340 --> 00:54:40.960
amp stacked floor to ceiling.

1095
00:54:40.960 --> 00:54:43.580
And I heard you say that on Dean's show too.

1096
00:54:43.580 --> 00:54:45.170
You're like, "I'm a terrible musician."

1097
00:54:45.170 --> 00:54:48.650
And that's kind of alcoholic thing to say, too,

1098
00:54:48.650 --> 00:54:50.900
because that's not an honest account

1099
00:54:50.900 --> 00:54:54.530
of who you are as a musician, I don't think.

1100
00:54:54.530 --> 00:54:55.442
Yeah.

1101
00:54:55.442 --> 00:54:56.810
And I don't think you believe that either,

1102
00:54:56.810 --> 00:54:58.080
or you wouldn't be trying to finish

1103
00:54:58.080 --> 00:55:00.330
this next album that you're trying to finish.

1104
00:55:02.522 --> 00:55:04.653
That's a tough question.

1105
00:55:08.195 --> 00:55:13.195
I keep coming back to music as something

1106
00:55:13.820 --> 00:55:16.160
that's incredibly meaningful in my life,

1107
00:55:16.160 --> 00:55:18.440
the same way that running is meaningful,

1108
00:55:18.440 --> 00:55:21.150
the same way that sobriety is meaningful is

1109
00:55:21.150 --> 00:55:26.150
that I can do it and do it and do it and do it

1110
00:55:28.330 --> 00:55:30.233
and never feel like I've beaten it.

1111
00:55:31.590 --> 00:55:34.870
I've found something bigger than me.

1112
00:55:34.870 --> 00:55:38.553
And that was a big thing when I was younger is just that,

1113
00:55:41.150 --> 00:55:42.793
even if I didn't win the fight,

1114
00:55:43.940 --> 00:55:46.670
I showed people that I could take a punch,

1115
00:55:46.670 --> 00:55:51.460
and that I could suffer really well.

1116
00:55:51.460 --> 00:55:56.450
I was good at those battles of attrition.

1117
00:55:56.450 --> 00:55:58.773
I would outlast other people.

1118
00:56:02.554 --> 00:56:03.387
And I-

1119
00:56:03.387 --> 00:56:06.980
But that's the power of the self-will,

1120
00:56:06.980 --> 00:56:09.560
this incredible self-will that you have,

1121
00:56:09.560 --> 00:56:14.440
that I think is germane to the alcoholic.

1122
00:56:14.440 --> 00:56:17.633
Yeah, it's funny,

1123
00:56:19.750 --> 00:56:23.770
being a real strong-headed guy,

1124
00:56:23.770 --> 00:56:25.490
I think that's what kept me drinking

1125
00:56:25.490 --> 00:56:28.110
for as long as I did in the face

1126
00:56:28.110 --> 00:56:31.630
of the mounting evidence that I was an alcoholic,

1127
00:56:31.630 --> 00:56:33.913
and that I had to stop.

1128
00:56:35.381 --> 00:56:36.737
When I finally stopped drinking,

1129
00:56:36.737 --> 00:56:38.860
people were like, "What happened?"

1130
00:56:38.860 --> 00:56:41.097
It must have been something really horrible.

1131
00:56:41.097 --> 00:56:42.187
And I was like,

1132
00:56:42.187 --> 00:56:45.420
"No, there wasn't really any one new, horrible thing.

1133
00:56:45.420 --> 00:56:48.570
It was just all the other stuff

1134
00:56:48.570 --> 00:56:51.900
that I'd ignored sort of finally sunk in,

1135
00:56:51.900 --> 00:56:56.900
that in no way was this a fulfilling life.

1136
00:56:57.690 --> 00:57:00.490
Right, you hear it all the time.

1137
00:57:00.490 --> 00:57:02.820
One day, you wake up and you're done.

1138
00:57:02.820 --> 00:57:05.120
It doesn't have to be some epic,

1139
00:57:05.120 --> 00:57:06.830
like a building doesn't have to fall on your head,

1140
00:57:06.830 --> 00:57:10.300
or you don't have to kill somebody in an auto accident.

1141
00:57:10.300 --> 00:57:12.620
You can just say I'm done.

1142
00:57:12.620 --> 00:57:13.610
Yeah, yeah.

1143
00:57:13.610 --> 00:57:15.210
And that's what happened for me.

1144
00:57:18.838 --> 00:57:21.510
I don't wanna say I've never craved a drink since then,

1145
00:57:21.510 --> 00:57:26.510
but it's like the spell was over,

1146
00:57:27.574 --> 00:57:32.574
and I could just see that I'd had every good night

1147
00:57:34.270 --> 00:57:36.450
on alcohol that I was gonna have,

1148
00:57:36.450 --> 00:57:39.350
and that the bad nights were just gonna repeat themselves.

1149
00:57:40.380 --> 00:57:43.260
Until I had one night that I wasn't gonna wake up from.

1150
00:57:43.260 --> 00:57:45.482
And I think it's a fallacy in recovery,

1151
00:57:45.482 --> 00:57:48.120
this idea that, now that you're sober,

1152
00:57:48.120 --> 00:57:50.410
you're not going to crave,

1153
00:57:50.410 --> 00:57:51.690
or there aren't gonna be moments

1154
00:57:51.690 --> 00:57:55.450
that are gonna be tricky to navigate.

1155
00:57:55.450 --> 00:58:00.450
The miracle is that an alcoholic isn't drunk all the time.

1156
00:58:01.930 --> 00:58:03.870
It's a miracle that you're not drinking.

1157
00:58:03.870 --> 00:58:06.510
And so I think it's important to acknowledge,

1158
00:58:06.510 --> 00:58:08.490
hey, I'm still an alcoholic.

1159
00:58:08.490 --> 00:58:10.163
I'm choosing not to drink today.

1160
00:58:11.180 --> 00:58:13.170
And some days are easier than others.

1161
00:58:13.170 --> 00:58:18.170
But this pressure to be this person

1162
00:58:19.930 --> 00:58:22.230
who holds themselves out to the world,

1163
00:58:22.230 --> 00:58:23.610
like I'm recovered and I don't think

1164
00:58:23.610 --> 00:58:25.580
about it anymore and all of that,

1165
00:58:25.580 --> 00:58:29.440
that's great, but that's not every day for me, for sure.

1166
00:58:29.440 --> 00:58:33.454
Yeah, I know

1167
00:58:33.454 --> 00:58:38.454
that my experience has been atypical,

1168
00:58:39.360 --> 00:58:42.160
and I know that there are people who struggle every day.

1169
00:58:43.409 --> 00:58:45.500
And man, my heart really goes out to them,

1170
00:58:45.500 --> 00:58:49.170
because for me, I think for me,

1171
00:58:49.170 --> 00:58:50.670
it's a lot more about sort of,

1172
00:58:51.520 --> 00:58:54.890
it's more about alcoholic behavior

1173
00:58:54.890 --> 00:58:57.943
than alcohol itself at this point.

1174
00:59:00.260 --> 00:59:04.830
I don't have to stop myself reaching for a glass

1175
00:59:04.830 --> 00:59:09.393
as much as I have to stop myself from having a thought,

1176
00:59:10.630 --> 00:59:12.763
or thinking in that way.

1177
00:59:16.658 --> 00:59:17.793
(coughs) One of my-

1178
00:59:18.850 --> 00:59:21.070
Thoughts and emotions are gonna happen though.

1179
00:59:21.070 --> 00:59:24.873
What's important is how you behave in reaction to those.

1180
00:59:25.720 --> 00:59:28.600
One of the things that I am really proud of is

1181
00:59:28.600 --> 00:59:33.600
that my mantra the last couple years

1182
00:59:33.840 --> 00:59:37.153
before I quit drinking was just fuck it.

1183
00:59:38.660 --> 00:59:39.880
Everybody's gotta die.

1184
00:59:39.880 --> 00:59:41.340
I've gotta die someday.

1185
00:59:41.340 --> 00:59:42.740
What day is it today, Monday?

1186
00:59:42.740 --> 00:59:46.510
Monday night, great night to go out, fuck it.

1187
00:59:46.510 --> 00:59:51.367
And now when I'm in traffic and somebody cuts me off,

1188
00:59:56.870 --> 01:00:00.360
or I knock one of the million guitars

1189
01:00:00.360 --> 01:00:02.320
that you see around the apartment,

1190
01:00:02.320 --> 01:00:05.890
I knock one of them over and it puts a dent

1191
01:00:05.890 --> 01:00:07.990
in the fingerboard or something like that.

1192
01:00:08.970 --> 01:00:12.623
I think, fuck it, it's not such a big deal.

1193
01:00:13.900 --> 01:00:18.173
And so I've been able to invert that.

1194
01:00:20.820 --> 01:00:23.160
But yeah, man, those thoughts still,

1195
01:00:23.160 --> 01:00:27.960
that's the thing is there is no firewall that's perfect.

1196
01:00:30.447 --> 01:00:33.200
And in fact, one of the things that I think

1197
01:00:36.290 --> 01:00:38.240
helped me in the long run

1198
01:00:39.805 --> 01:00:44.143
with quitting alcohol was working in a bar.

1199
01:00:45.120 --> 01:00:49.163
Because every night that I worked,

1200
01:00:50.030 --> 01:00:53.050
there was the entire alphabet

1201
01:00:53.050 --> 01:00:56.600
of alcohol there available for me free,

1202
01:00:56.600 --> 01:00:59.403
the alpha and the omega of alcohol.

1203
01:01:02.440 --> 01:01:07.440
So I knew that I wasn't avoiding temptation.

1204
01:01:07.660 --> 01:01:08.963
That it was sort of like,

1205
01:01:09.980 --> 01:01:11.930
'cause that's the thing is if you want a drink, man,

1206
01:01:11.930 --> 01:01:14.130
you'll find a drink, whether it's vanilla extract,

1207
01:01:14.130 --> 01:01:16.780
or rubbing alcohol, or mouthwash.

1208
01:01:16.780 --> 01:01:18.220
If you're determined to get a drink,

1209
01:01:18.220 --> 01:01:19.670
there's always one available.

1210
01:01:21.315 --> 01:01:22.160
So seeing it there,

1211
01:01:22.160 --> 01:01:24.710
seeing it all right there in front of me, that was,

1212
01:01:27.150 --> 01:01:30.570
it made it clear that the change had happened in me,

1213
01:01:30.570 --> 01:01:33.320
that it wasn't just a situational thing.

1214
01:01:33.320 --> 01:01:37.340
And then also, towards the end of my shift

1215
01:01:37.340 --> 01:01:38.173
working at the bar,

1216
01:01:38.173 --> 01:01:41.263
when you see people just behaving horribly,

1217
01:01:42.370 --> 01:01:46.140
I was like-
The romance is no longer.

1218
01:01:46.140 --> 01:01:48.820
Yeah, I was like, oh yeah, this is done.

1219
01:01:48.820 --> 01:01:50.083
This is totally over.

1220
01:01:51.230 --> 01:01:52.550
All right, I gotta go to the bathroom.

1221
01:01:52.550 --> 01:01:53.400
Hold on a second.

1222
01:01:53.400 --> 01:01:55.008
We gotta pause.
()

1223
01:01:55.008 --> 01:01:57.700
Please hang up and try again.

1224
01:01:57.700 --> 01:01:59.860
I feel much better for the break.

1225
01:02:01.010 --> 01:02:05.053
But no, I wanted to circle it back to,

1226
01:02:05.053 --> 01:02:07.990
you were kind of making this distinction

1227
01:02:07.990 --> 01:02:09.540
between your musical talent

1228
01:02:09.540 --> 01:02:11.560
and your writing talent and how music,

1229
01:02:11.560 --> 01:02:13.660
essentially what you were saying, correct me if I'm wrong,

1230
01:02:13.660 --> 01:02:15.860
is that music comes hard to you

1231
01:02:15.860 --> 01:02:17.897
and you have to really apply yourself,

1232
01:02:17.897 --> 01:02:20.530
and you know that there's sort of a glass ceiling

1233
01:02:20.530 --> 01:02:22.570
for you in terms of your innate talent,

1234
01:02:22.570 --> 01:02:25.660
but that with writing, you're finally accepting

1235
01:02:25.660 --> 01:02:29.710
that you do have a gift for this,

1236
01:02:29.710 --> 01:02:31.320
and that it comes easier for you.

1237
01:02:31.320 --> 01:02:35.000
And you're embracing that in a, I guess in a new way.

1238
01:02:35.000 --> 01:02:37.003
Yeah, I think that,

1239
01:02:38.060 --> 01:02:39.660
we talk a little bit about,

1240
01:02:39.660 --> 01:02:41.120
sort of going out and boozing it up

1241
01:02:41.120 --> 01:02:46.120
and still doing fine academically.

1242
01:02:46.160 --> 01:02:51.160
And when I was younger, it made me think-

1243
01:02:51.190 --> 01:02:52.023
By the way,

1244
01:02:52.023 --> 01:02:53.640
I'm gonna interrupt you just for a second.

1245
01:02:53.640 --> 01:02:58.410
So do you get shit for coming off arrogant

1246
01:02:58.410 --> 01:03:00.700
in your account of that?

1247
01:03:00.700 --> 01:03:03.200
Like, "Oh, I'm supposed to feel sorry for this guy?

1248
01:03:03.200 --> 01:03:04.980
He got into Columbia when he was a drunk."

1249
01:03:04.980 --> 01:03:07.506
Do you get like blowback?

1250
01:03:07.506 --> 01:03:10.250
(laughs) If I ever have

1251
01:03:10.250 --> 01:03:12.070
the good fortune to teach a writing class,

1252
01:03:12.070 --> 01:03:14.360
the first writing or the first reading assignment

1253
01:03:14.360 --> 01:03:15.580
will be for people to read

1254
01:03:15.580 --> 01:03:17.810
all my one star reviews on Amazon.

1255
01:03:17.810 --> 01:03:19.110
Right, I got a few myself, too.

1256
01:03:19.110 --> 01:03:21.970
I got some really, really nasty ones from people.

1257
01:03:21.970 --> 01:03:25.040
My favorite, I'm gonna, this is so great.

1258
01:03:25.040 --> 01:03:26.890
I read it to my kids the other day.

1259
01:03:26.890 --> 01:03:29.807
My favorite one star review on my book is,

1260
01:03:29.807 --> 01:03:33.600
"This book is fantastic if you're George Bush."

1261
01:03:33.600 --> 01:03:35.710
And it goes on to talk about how I had

1262
01:03:35.710 --> 01:03:38.730
this crazy privileged background

1263
01:03:38.730 --> 01:03:41.830
where my dad bailed me out of every pitfall

1264
01:03:41.830 --> 01:03:43.713
that I ever had my whole life and took care of it.

1265
01:03:43.713 --> 01:03:46.850
It's like the way that this guy projected

1266
01:03:46.850 --> 01:03:50.190
whatever baggage he was carrying onto my life.

1267
01:03:50.190 --> 01:03:52.590
And I'm like, when I told my story was like,

1268
01:03:52.590 --> 01:03:53.850
I can't get around the,

1269
01:03:53.850 --> 01:03:57.430
I grew up in a very nurturing household.

1270
01:03:57.430 --> 01:03:58.406
I had-

1271
01:03:58.406 --> 01:03:59.239
And you should never apologize.

1272
01:03:59.239 --> 01:04:00.720
And I'm not gonna apologize,

1273
01:04:00.720 --> 01:04:02.190
and I'm not going to,

1274
01:04:02.190 --> 01:04:05.230
for the sake of creating additional drama,

1275
01:04:05.230 --> 01:04:06.897
take poetic license with that,

1276
01:04:06.897 --> 01:04:08.760
and make it sound like something that it wasn't.

1277
01:04:08.760 --> 01:04:09.800
It just is what it is.

1278
01:04:09.800 --> 01:04:11.840
And some people will connect with that,

1279
01:04:11.840 --> 01:04:15.010
and other people won't, but I've taken flak for that.

1280
01:04:15.010 --> 01:04:16.750
Like, oh, you went to Stanford,

1281
01:04:16.750 --> 01:04:18.600
and I'm supposed to feel bad for you?

1282
01:04:19.440 --> 01:04:21.427
Yeah, the Columbia thing,

1283
01:04:21.427 --> 01:04:23.740
people make a big deal about it.

1284
01:04:23.740 --> 01:04:28.720
And I'm still $90,000 in debt from Columbia.

1285
01:04:28.720 --> 01:04:31.833
So if anybody's jealous of me,

1286
01:04:33.240 --> 01:04:35.290
I'm willing to trade places with you on that one.

1287
01:04:35.290 --> 01:04:36.123
Come on, man.

1288
01:04:36.123 --> 01:04:37.560
You're supposed to be here telling me

1289
01:04:37.560 --> 01:04:40.794
how you're hammering these huge Amazon checks every day

1290
01:04:40.794 --> 01:04:42.340
that come in the mail in a daily basis.

1291
01:04:42.340 --> 01:04:44.860
Dude, look around the apartment.

1292
01:04:44.860 --> 01:04:47.490
Where do you think it's going?

1293
01:04:47.490 --> 01:04:48.400
All right, but to get back.

1294
01:04:48.400 --> 01:04:51.850
So sort of embracing this talent that you have.

1295
01:04:51.850 --> 01:04:56.270
And by the way, I just retweeted today,

1296
01:04:56.270 --> 01:04:58.712
the article that you put up on,

1297
01:04:58.712 --> 01:05:02.500
the iReports on CNN about running a marathon

1298
01:05:02.500 --> 01:05:06.940
on Sunday to honor the Boston victims.

1299
01:05:06.940 --> 01:05:09.840
And I wanna talk about that in a little bit,

1300
01:05:09.840 --> 01:05:14.770
but that could have been a very bland post

1301
01:05:14.770 --> 01:05:16.410
that just said I'm gonna get out there.

1302
01:05:16.410 --> 01:05:18.280
It could have been a very straight up the middle.

1303
01:05:18.280 --> 01:05:19.820
But it's very clear.

1304
01:05:19.820 --> 01:05:21.860
And I don't know how much time you spent writing that,

1305
01:05:21.860 --> 01:05:23.370
but you read that and you're like,

1306
01:05:23.370 --> 01:05:25.800
oh, this is not like just an average blog post.

1307
01:05:25.800 --> 01:05:29.950
This is beautifully composed the way that you wrote that.

1308
01:05:29.950 --> 01:05:31.300
Well, thank you.

1309
01:05:33.860 --> 01:05:35.773
This is the thing, is that like,

1310
01:05:38.050 --> 01:05:41.253
I'm probably drunk from like 15 to 32,

1311
01:05:43.660 --> 01:05:46.130
so through most of my academic career.

1312
01:05:46.130 --> 01:05:51.130
And so a typical thing is I would go out

1313
01:05:52.450 --> 01:05:56.650
and get wasted and then spend an hour writing a paper

1314
01:05:56.650 --> 01:05:59.670
and turn it in thinking this is total garbage.

1315
01:05:59.670 --> 01:06:02.360
And then my professor would write something saying like,

1316
01:06:02.360 --> 01:06:04.370
this is a fantastic paper.

1317
01:06:04.370 --> 01:06:08.410
this really moved me, or new insights or whatever.

1318
01:06:08.410 --> 01:06:11.690
And would take that feedback and say,

1319
01:06:11.690 --> 01:06:14.900
oh, well, this is proof that the world is meaningless.

1320
01:06:14.900 --> 01:06:17.100
If I can crank this out

1321
01:06:17.100 --> 01:06:20.880
on a couple hours and impress people,

1322
01:06:20.880 --> 01:06:25.880
then it's proof that literature doesn't exist,

1323
01:06:26.550 --> 01:06:27.660
or that trying to communicate

1324
01:06:27.660 --> 01:06:31.060
through these little squiggles is meaningless.

1325
01:06:31.060 --> 01:06:33.420
Well, and what it is act in actuality is

1326
01:06:33.420 --> 01:06:36.060
just incredibly poor self-esteem.

1327
01:06:36.060 --> 01:06:41.060
That your talent still was able to transcend

1328
01:06:41.170 --> 01:06:42.990
that low self-esteem to be able

1329
01:06:42.990 --> 01:06:45.453
to deliver prose on that level.

1330
01:06:46.830 --> 01:06:49.070
Rich, the next nice thing you say about me,

1331
01:06:49.070 --> 01:06:50.560
I'm gonna give you a charley horse,

1332
01:06:50.560 --> 01:06:52.780
'cause I can't deal with it.

1333
01:06:52.780 --> 01:06:54.250
It's like, this is the thing, man.

1334
01:06:54.250 --> 01:06:56.350
That's your low self-esteem right there.

1335
01:06:56.350 --> 01:06:57.440
I know, this is the thing.

1336
01:06:57.440 --> 01:06:59.150
If you were just giving me

1337
01:06:59.150 --> 01:07:01.990
a series of direct personal attack,

1338
01:07:01.990 --> 01:07:05.530
I can handle that way better than I can handle you saying,

1339
01:07:05.530 --> 01:07:07.410
somebody whose work I admire

1340
01:07:07.410 --> 01:07:10.950
and whose accomplishments I'm envious of

1341
01:07:10.950 --> 01:07:13.100
to sit on my coach in my filthy apartment

1342
01:07:13.100 --> 01:07:14.600
and say nice things about me.

1343
01:07:14.600 --> 01:07:17.310
That's a specific hell right there.

1344
01:07:17.310 --> 01:07:21.812
I'm talking to the guy who's been on "The Moth."

1345
01:07:21.812 --> 01:07:26.300
You've done some amazing things, man.

1346
01:07:26.300 --> 01:07:27.623
Just own it.

1347
01:07:27.623 --> 01:07:29.410
I know, I can't yet.

1348
01:07:29.410 --> 01:07:30.243
I can't.

1349
01:07:30.243 --> 01:07:31.076
It's,

1350
01:07:33.000 --> 01:07:35.120
let me back it up.
All right.

1351
01:07:35.120 --> 01:07:38.629
So to get this great feedback

1352
01:07:38.629 --> 01:07:43.629
from my professors at esteemed academic institutions,

1353
01:07:43.730 --> 01:07:46.810
I would just think, oh, it's all bullshit.

1354
01:07:46.810 --> 01:07:50.250
When really now I'm getting to a point

1355
01:07:50.250 --> 01:07:55.060
where I can go back and say, no, I was gifted.

1356
01:07:57.396 --> 01:07:59.110
And and I am gifted,

1357
01:07:59.110 --> 01:08:02.940
and I have a gift to be able to write well,

1358
01:08:02.940 --> 01:08:07.940
and I seem to be grateful for it and appreciate it.

1359
01:08:08.200 --> 01:08:12.810
And it's a lot to learn, man.

1360
01:08:12.810 --> 01:08:14.863
Learning something like that, it's not,

1361
01:08:17.330 --> 01:08:19.440
the paradigm is not losing your virginity,

1362
01:08:19.440 --> 01:08:20.900
like you do it once and then it's done.

1363
01:08:20.900 --> 01:08:23.380
It's like making your bed.

1364
01:08:23.380 --> 01:08:27.313
I have to teach that thing to myself every day.

1365
01:08:28.180 --> 01:08:29.410
I forget it, and then I'm like,

1366
01:08:29.410 --> 01:08:31.780
oh yeah, I have a gift for writing,

1367
01:08:31.780 --> 01:08:34.610
it's great, I need to appreciate it.

1368
01:08:34.610 --> 01:08:36.700
I have some merit as a human being.

1369
01:08:36.700 --> 01:08:37.900
I need to remember that.

1370
01:08:38.780 --> 01:08:42.850
I'm probably not gonna end up electing the carts

1371
01:08:42.850 --> 01:08:46.400
at a grocery store or working as a squeegee man,

1372
01:08:46.400 --> 01:08:49.023
which is how I assumed my life would end.

1373
01:08:51.340 --> 01:08:52.210
And so do you feel

1374
01:08:52.210 --> 01:08:55.203
an added sense of responsibility to tend to that?

1375
01:08:58.637 --> 01:08:59.470
To tend

1376
01:08:59.470 --> 01:09:01.312
to my own sense of self-worth?
Well, because,

1377
01:09:01.312 --> 01:09:05.380
no, to kind of get to a place where you're in acceptance

1378
01:09:05.380 --> 01:09:08.050
of this talent that you have and say, okay,

1379
01:09:08.050 --> 01:09:11.250
well, this talent comes with responsibility.

1380
01:09:11.250 --> 01:09:15.440
It's my responsibility to pursue this seriously

1381
01:09:15.440 --> 01:09:18.873
and lean into it as opposed to deflect it.

1382
01:09:23.810 --> 01:09:26.740
Well, I think there's a third option.

1383
01:09:26.740 --> 01:09:31.740
My father was sort of, he's a very gifted physicist.

1384
01:09:32.100 --> 01:09:36.770
And he was sort of oppressed by his father as a child,

1385
01:09:36.770 --> 01:09:38.410
saying like, "You have this gift.

1386
01:09:38.410 --> 01:09:40.670
With that gift, you have this responsibility.

1387
01:09:40.670 --> 01:09:41.810
And you have to do this.

1388
01:09:41.810 --> 01:09:44.050
You have to take care of other people."

1389
01:09:44.050 --> 01:09:47.390
And I guess the way that I try to frame it is

1390
01:09:47.390 --> 01:09:51.230
that I have an awesome opportunity,

1391
01:09:51.230 --> 01:09:54.470
and I'm just waking up to it now, man,

1392
01:09:54.470 --> 01:09:59.370
that I can have this life where I go

1393
01:09:59.370 --> 01:10:03.570
and do crazy races with my friends and hang out,

1394
01:10:03.570 --> 01:10:06.730
and eat watermelon on the trail,

1395
01:10:06.730 --> 01:10:10.610
running the Finger Lakes 50s with some amazing runners.

1396
01:10:10.610 --> 01:10:14.910
And I get to hang out in my underwear all day

1397
01:10:14.910 --> 01:10:17.410
and basically write the equivalent

1398
01:10:17.410 --> 01:10:20.733
of a long, funny email to my mom,

1399
01:10:21.640 --> 01:10:26.110
and people will read it, and people will dig it.

1400
01:10:26.110 --> 01:10:29.173
And so I'm trying to just say,

1401
01:10:31.400 --> 01:10:33.410
trying to learn to go with the flow, man,

1402
01:10:33.410 --> 01:10:36.723
and say, oh, work to your strengths.

1403
01:10:39.805 --> 01:10:42.020
What do you think is,

1404
01:10:42.020 --> 01:10:44.690
I'm obsessed with creative people.

1405
01:10:44.690 --> 01:10:45.940
I'm obsessed with artists.

1406
01:10:45.940 --> 01:10:50.863
And there's definitely a jealousy aspect of that going on.

1407
01:10:52.240 --> 01:10:55.340
And I find that in recovery,

1408
01:10:55.340 --> 01:10:57.850
I just meet some of the most brilliant,

1409
01:10:57.850 --> 01:11:00.773
creative people that I've ever met.

1410
01:11:02.592 --> 01:11:07.500
And I'm interested in your thoughts on,

1411
01:11:07.500 --> 01:11:12.440
there's this nexus between the romanticization

1412
01:11:12.440 --> 01:11:15.341
of using drugs and alcohol,

1413
01:11:15.341 --> 01:11:18.900
and how that fosters creativity.

1414
01:11:18.900 --> 01:11:23.453
Or this sort of misidentification of that relationship,

1415
01:11:24.780 --> 01:11:27.210
and then getting sober and saying, "Oh my God,

1416
01:11:27.210 --> 01:11:28.880
I'm never gonna have another creative thought.

1417
01:11:28.880 --> 01:11:33.370
How am I gonna be an artist without this crutch

1418
01:11:33.370 --> 01:11:36.477
that I thought was stimulating all of my creativity?"

1419
01:11:37.670 --> 01:11:42.630
Yeah, as a musician and as a songwriter,

1420
01:11:42.630 --> 01:11:47.630
I certainly built all my writing around alcohol,

1421
01:11:48.520 --> 01:11:51.842
and around being a drunk,

1422
01:11:51.842 --> 01:11:56.333
and around drinking and failing.

1423
01:11:59.560 --> 01:12:01.116
Hold on, I'm gonna close the door.

1424
01:12:01.116 --> 01:12:02.660
All right, no problem.

1425
01:12:02.660 --> 01:12:05.090
So as a musician and as a songwriter,

1426
01:12:05.090 --> 01:12:10.090
I really built my sense of self around alcohol and failure,

1427
01:12:11.940 --> 01:12:13.983
which were like my two sidekicks.

1428
01:12:15.460 --> 01:12:20.460
And then now I find myself in a really bizarre situation

1429
01:12:20.600 --> 01:12:23.870
where I think I have an audience of people

1430
01:12:23.870 --> 01:12:25.873
who would be interested in my music.

1431
01:12:27.970 --> 01:12:32.970
And I really feel weird about playing my old stuff,

1432
01:12:34.370 --> 01:12:37.810
and I'm not writing a lot of new stuff.

1433
01:12:37.810 --> 01:12:42.810
So yeah, that's a corner that I've yet to turn.

1434
01:12:44.720 --> 01:12:47.930
What I hear all the time is

1435
01:12:47.930 --> 01:12:51.173
that there is that fear, and then ultimately,

1436
01:12:52.770 --> 01:12:54.850
with the foundation of sobriety,

1437
01:12:54.850 --> 01:12:57.760
these artists are able to become creative fonts

1438
01:12:57.760 --> 01:12:59.160
far in excess of anything

1439
01:12:59.160 --> 01:13:01.820
that they thought they would be prior.

1440
01:13:01.820 --> 01:13:06.030
And despite grappling with this idea

1441
01:13:06.030 --> 01:13:08.450
that I'm never gonna be able to create again

1442
01:13:08.450 --> 01:13:12.340
without my drug of choice.

1443
01:13:12.340 --> 01:13:16.360
Yeah, my writing has certainly exploded,

1444
01:13:16.360 --> 01:13:20.410
and I'm so grateful for that.

1445
01:13:20.410 --> 01:13:21.243
And actually, you know what?

1446
01:13:21.243 --> 01:13:22.100
This is really funny.

1447
01:13:22.100 --> 01:13:23.500
This is an interesting thing.

1448
01:13:23.500 --> 01:13:26.810
I sent out a book proposal recently, which,

1449
01:13:26.810 --> 01:13:28.090
there was all this hype going into it,

1450
01:13:28.090 --> 01:13:30.620
and I was like, oh, awesome, I'm gonna get a book deal.

1451
01:13:30.620 --> 01:13:31.470
Didn't happen.

1452
01:13:31.470 --> 01:13:33.730
Didn't get a single bit on the project.

1453
01:13:33.730 --> 01:13:35.930
And it was heartbreaking.

1454
01:13:35.930 --> 01:13:40.590
And I went to my kickboxing class and beat some people up.

1455
01:13:40.590 --> 01:13:45.590
And then I was driving back and I was like,

1456
01:13:46.860 --> 01:13:49.760
I'm still crazy about this.

1457
01:13:49.760 --> 01:13:51.083
What am I gonna do now?

1458
01:13:52.670 --> 01:13:56.113
Am I gonna go out and run until the sun comes up?

1459
01:13:57.280 --> 01:13:59.300
And then the whole next day will be shot?

1460
01:13:59.300 --> 01:14:01.850
Am I gonna go and take a drink?

1461
01:14:01.850 --> 01:14:03.050
No.

1462
01:14:03.050 --> 01:14:05.800
And I just came home, and I showered.

1463
01:14:05.800 --> 01:14:09.230
And then there was a song that my friend had sent me

1464
01:14:09.230 --> 01:14:13.060
that he wanted me to write lyrics and do vocals for,

1465
01:14:13.060 --> 01:14:15.130
that I've been putting off and putting off and putting off

1466
01:14:15.130 --> 01:14:18.540
because of this writing, because of the book proposal.

1467
01:14:18.540 --> 01:14:20.440
I finished the song and I recorded it.

1468
01:14:21.480 --> 01:14:24.563
And I didn't really think much of it until the next morning.

1469
01:14:24.563 --> 01:14:27.964
Then I was like, oh, okay.

1470
01:14:27.964 --> 01:14:31.870
I got this really negative information

1471
01:14:31.870 --> 01:14:33.700
that I'm not gonna sell a book proposal

1472
01:14:33.700 --> 01:14:36.143
I was counting on selling,

1473
01:14:37.740 --> 01:14:42.630
and I was able to convert that into another creative project

1474
01:14:42.630 --> 01:14:45.950
and harness that energy for creativity.

1475
01:14:45.950 --> 01:14:47.620
Right, and the bigger issue

1476
01:14:47.620 --> 01:14:52.620
at play really is having a healthy relationship

1477
01:14:53.440 --> 01:14:55.380
with things that you can control

1478
01:14:55.380 --> 01:14:57.120
and things that you can't.

1479
01:14:57.120 --> 01:14:58.930
So you have no control

1480
01:14:58.930 --> 01:15:01.250
over other people's reaction to your work,

1481
01:15:01.250 --> 01:15:04.120
whether it's a five-star review on Amazon or a one-star.

1482
01:15:04.120 --> 01:15:05.880
Or whether a big publishing house

1483
01:15:05.880 --> 01:15:08.150
wants to buy your book or not.

1484
01:15:08.150 --> 01:15:10.250
Those are things that are none of your business.

1485
01:15:10.250 --> 01:15:12.860
The only thing that you have control over is

1486
01:15:12.860 --> 01:15:15.230
the quality of the work

1487
01:15:15.230 --> 01:15:17.440
that you're putting down on paper and putting out there,

1488
01:15:17.440 --> 01:15:21.790
or putting on a CD with your music or an MP3.

1489
01:15:21.790 --> 01:15:24.253
So everything else is,

1490
01:15:26.110 --> 01:15:27.243
they say it in recovery all the time,

1491
01:15:27.243 --> 01:15:28.860
it's not your business.

1492
01:15:28.860 --> 01:15:31.060
And detaching from that is

1493
01:15:31.060 --> 01:15:33.550
where the hard work has to come in.

1494
01:15:33.550 --> 01:15:36.196
And I have friends that do the same thing all the time.

1495
01:15:36.196 --> 01:15:40.700
They become attached to a certain outcome

1496
01:15:40.700 --> 01:15:42.380
with respect to a project they're working on.

1497
01:15:42.380 --> 01:15:44.370
It doesn't go that way.

1498
01:15:44.370 --> 01:15:46.370
Or you're sitting around waiting.

1499
01:15:46.370 --> 01:15:48.620
Like you sent your proposal out.

1500
01:15:48.620 --> 01:15:49.683
How long did you wait before they're

1501
01:15:49.683 --> 01:15:50.981
getting back to you?
Oh God,

1502
01:15:50.981 --> 01:15:51.814
yeah, that's the worst thing.

1503
01:15:51.814 --> 01:15:54.730
And that's, you have no control.

1504
01:15:54.730 --> 01:15:57.120
Or you're an actor who's auditioned for a part,

1505
01:15:57.120 --> 01:15:58.490
and you're sitting around waiting,

1506
01:15:58.490 --> 01:16:01.950
knowing that if you get it, your life changes forever.

1507
01:16:01.950 --> 01:16:03.440
Yeah, and in those situations,

1508
01:16:03.440 --> 01:16:04.640
your mind just turns on you.

1509
01:16:04.640 --> 01:16:06.660
Your mind just starts to consume you.

1510
01:16:06.660 --> 01:16:08.257
And it's horrible.

1511
01:16:08.257 --> 01:16:09.900
Yeah, it's not a healthy head space,

1512
01:16:09.900 --> 01:16:13.180
so the only thing you can do is like, oh, I'll go create,

1513
01:16:13.180 --> 01:16:17.140
the only way you can exercise any control over whatsoever,

1514
01:16:17.140 --> 01:16:19.730
over your destiny, being a creative person

1515
01:16:19.730 --> 01:16:22.100
who makes a living off things that are coming out

1516
01:16:22.100 --> 01:16:25.230
of your brain is to just create something new.

1517
01:16:25.230 --> 01:16:27.380
Yeah, and I said this

1518
01:16:27.380 --> 01:16:30.093
to a standup comic friend last night,

1519
01:16:31.140 --> 01:16:34.870
which is to pursue a life

1520
01:16:34.870 --> 01:16:39.240
in which you have a creative profession is

1521
01:16:39.240 --> 01:16:41.363
to become a professional failure.

1522
01:16:42.300 --> 01:16:44.330
You're gonna fail more often-

1523
01:16:44.330 --> 01:16:46.890
Of course.
Than you're gonna succeed.

1524
01:16:46.890 --> 01:16:49.440
And you have to be okay with that.

1525
01:16:49.440 --> 01:16:53.090
The successes have to be meaningful enough

1526
01:16:53.090 --> 01:16:55.539
that it's gonna sustain you through the failure,

1527
01:16:55.539 --> 01:16:57.553
and that you can deal with it.

1528
01:16:58.894 --> 01:17:03.436
The analogy is, it's like making a baby.

1529
01:17:03.436 --> 01:17:08.436
Only one of those sperms needs to hit the egg.

1530
01:17:09.169 --> 01:17:10.250
Right, every other one of them fail.

1531
01:17:10.250 --> 01:17:11.670
Yeah, and you're good.

1532
01:17:11.670 --> 01:17:12.870
You just gotta land one.

1533
01:17:13.820 --> 01:17:18.820
Right, and truth be told, 15 years ago,

1534
01:17:18.890 --> 01:17:21.440
you sent a book proposal out, you don't get a deal,

1535
01:17:21.440 --> 01:17:22.810
that's it, dude, you're done.

1536
01:17:22.810 --> 01:17:26.943
But you're Mr. Number One Kindle Singles Guy.

1537
01:17:29.199 --> 01:17:31.340
You can own your distribution method.

1538
01:17:31.340 --> 01:17:32.890
You have an audience.

1539
01:17:32.890 --> 01:17:36.040
And so you don't need that gatekeeper

1540
01:17:36.040 --> 01:17:38.940
to give you the stamp of approval over your work.

1541
01:17:38.940 --> 01:17:41.030
You still have the ability to,

1542
01:17:41.030 --> 01:17:43.690
should you choose, to publish that.

1543
01:17:43.690 --> 01:17:46.020
And you know that there are people out there

1544
01:17:46.020 --> 01:17:48.770
that are interested in hearing what you have to say.

1545
01:17:48.770 --> 01:17:51.220
Well, and you phrased that in the right way.

1546
